INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7121,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351251','Exploring communication by public health leaders and organizations during the pandemic: a content analysis of COVID-related tweets.','James L, McPhail H, Foisey L, Donelle L, Bauer M, Kothari A.','2023','essentielle medias sociaux connectivite responsables outils numeriques exploites porte publies responsables organismes mondiale comprendre lutter urgences sanitaires urgents contenus publies aout canadien servi responsables constats responsables organismes porte faibles cernes responsables eventail limite abordes limite envergure profondeur renforcement pandemies sanitaires faudrait pousser determiner responsables organismes exemplaires toutes plateformes medias sociaux differentes mots cles pandemies medias sociaux numeriques edo osagie neiger gruzd usership slavik cerc kothari cerc detsky bogoch haman elo kyngas cpho cphos bonnie drbonniehenry fnha cdcofbc phsaofbc goahealth deena hinshaw cmoh_alberta ahs_behealthy skgov saskhealth mbgov roussin roussin_brent onthealth publichealthon tbaymoh nbhealth hcs_govnl janice cmoh_nl healthns nshealth strang strangrobert health_pei kami kandola cpho nwt nwt_cpho nthssa hssyukon govofnunavut govcanhealth cpho_canada keshteli polisena premium rossum tweepy searchtweets pigott roesslein outgoing programmatically replying pandas dispelling closings daycare curfews reinstatement webchat praise dispelling qsr bonnie nunavut disinfecting fomites depictions yukon celebration praised thanked reaffirmed complying advisories overburdened showcased hardship showcased tightening loosening tweeted weblink yukon newfoundland yukon nunavut tworek slavik disempowers defund mismanagement speedy tworek elon musk takeover bans outages outsiders cerc memoing spotlights metre metres abtracetogether albertarelaunch aparttogether aplanirlacourbe aplanissonslacourbe metres autoisolement autoisolezvous cerb chinavirus closecanadianborders closetheborder closetheschools cohorting contacttracing covd19 covetiquette covi19 covid_tag_roots_infection reducethespread deconfinement disinfect distanciationphysique distanciationsociale dontbeaspreader eloignementphysique flatten flattened flattenthecurve flattenthecuve reopen handsanitizers birthday birthday birthday healthyathome helppreventthespread transmi inthistogether isolat houshold physicaldistance physicaldistancing distan plankthecurve quarantineofficers resuming transmi celebrate celebrate isloate selfmonitor selfmonitoring slowthespread socialdistance socialdistancing metres stayathome stayhome stayinghome stayinsideyourbubble stopchinaflights stopthespread strongertogether testandtrace togetherapart tracetogether travelrestrictions metres kothari foisey donelle mcphail foisey shortt thune brønnick lungu røislien wiig 121043000235_1 cerc cerc cerc_introduction detsky bogoch labbe malo s41997 edo osagie iglesia edeghere compbiomed elo kyngas snook gambhir zth gruzd sp2 xiw8ew haman s0008423921000020 snider haire joshu pcd10 moreland choucair staub tweeting e238 vijaykumar pubrev keshteli anjum sivakumaran dib gagliardi lacarte e0226489 kothari foisey donelle e041818 aldosery vitiugin novillo kostkova augustine strikes issac conger outages outages elon musk colangeli gillis mcwhirter papadopoulos ijerph18157986 majora 92bf1922 00a workbook neiger thackeray wagenen fagen neiger thackeray giraud fagen elon musk fired elon musk pigott montague searchtweets pypi searchtweets polisena sanni matenchuk livergant amjad zoric bogoch e046177 qsr qsr pty qsrinternational roesslein tweepy tweepy tweepy slavik darlington buttle sturrock yiannakoulias tweeting geographies healthplace e26720 tisseverasinghe e175 e194 tworek beacock ojo democracy2017 olt covid_final rossum scotts createspace renshaw butts retweeting risa staub tweeting jphmp e24 defunding theatlantic defund 20international 20travellers 20hand 20hygiene 20frequently 20particularly 20alcohol 2dbased 20hand 20rub','Abstract Objectives Health communication is an essential competency in public health practice. The increasing use of social media and the connectivity between the general public and public health leaders present a unique opportunity to explore how digital communications tools were leveraged in the COVID-19 pandemic. This study explores Twitter-based communications from public health leaders and organizations across Canada and compares them with those from the World Health Organization (WHO). This research aimed to understand Twitter communications strategies to address the COVID-19 pandemic, other public health emergencies, and non-emergency public health issues. Methods A content analysis of COVID-related Twitter content during the first wave of the pandemic (January 1–August 31, 2020) was performed. The Canadian Institute for Health Information (CIHI) Policy Intervention Scan was used as a framework to analyze messaging from public health leaders and the WHO. Results Findings demonstrate that most tweets from public health leaders and organizations in Canada and the WHO focused on case management and public information. Gaps and areas of weakness identified include the lack of Twitter participation by some public health leaders and a narrow range of policy intervention topics, limiting the breadth and depth of public health messages. Conclusion Strengthening communications can serve to improve information sharing in future pandemics or public health crises. Further research should assess how public health leaders and organizations applied communication best practices on all social media platforms and across different policy interventions. Keywords: Pandemic response, Social media, Digital communications, Public health leadership, Public health emergency Résumé Objectifs La communication de messages sur la santé est une compétence essentielle de la pratique en santé publique. L’utilisation accrue des médias sociaux et de la connectivité entre le grand public et les responsables de la santé publique présente une occasion unique d’explorer comment les outils de communication numériques ont été exploités pendant la pandémie de COVID-19. Notre étude porte sur les messages publiés sur Twitter par les responsables et les organismes de la santé publique du Canada et les compare à ceux de l’Organisation mondiale de la santé (OMS). Notre but était de comprendre les stratégies de communication sur Twitter employées pour lutter contre la pandémie de COVID-19, les autres urgences sanitaires et les problèmes de santé publique non urgents. Méthode Nous avons analysé les contenus liés à la COVID-19 publiés sur Twitter au cours du premier cycle de la pandémie (1 er janvier au 31 août 2020). L’outil d’analyse des interventions de l’Institut canadien d’information sur la santé (ICIS) a servi de cadre à l’analyse des messages des responsables de la santé publique et de l’OMS. Résultats Selon nos constats, la plupart des messages des responsables et des organismes de la santé publique du Canada et de l’OMS ont porté sur la gestion des cas et l’information publique. Les lacunes et les points faibles cernés ont été l’absence de certains responsables de la santé publique sur Twitter et l’éventail limité de sujets abordés; ces deux éléments ont limité l’envergure et la profondeur des messages de santé publique. Conclusion Le renforcement des communications pourrait améliorer la communication d’informations lors de futures pandémies ou crises sanitaires. Il faudrait pousser la recherche pour déterminer si les responsables et les organismes de la santé publique ont utilisé des pratiques de communication exemplaires sur toutes les plateformes de médias sociaux et pour différentes interventions. Mots-clés: Lutte contre les pandémies, médias sociaux, communications numériques, leadership en santé publique, urgence sanitaire Introduction One of the key competencies and responsibilities of public health officials is to develop and deliver health communications, including emergency response messages, to the public (Jin et al., 2019 ). During public health emergencies, such as the COVID-19 pandemic, the public relies on public health officials to offer accurate and up-to-date information to aid in decision-making about health and facilitate a sense of security and trust in government (Vos et al., 2018 ). Canadian performance indicators for public health emergency preparedness also emphasize the significance of communication including skill with social media, monitoring online misinformation/disinformation, and engaging with individuals’ questions and concerns (Khan et al., 2019 ). Social media serves as a pivotal communication tool as it connects public health leaders to the communities they serve in real time (Vos et al., 2018 ). Previous work has found that Twitter is a frequently used social media platform by public health authorities (Booth et al., 2017 ; Edo-Osagie et al., 2020 ; Neiger et al., 2012 , 2013 ). Approximately 15 million Canadians use Twitter monthly and half are on Twitter daily, demonstrating a wide reach and strong frequency of engagement with the platform. Existing evidence points to Twitter as an effective communications channel during disease outbreaks (Gruzd & Mai, 2020 ). Since the outset of the COVID-19 pandemic, Twitter usership has increased, possibly a result of people seeking current and ever-evolving health information at a time of ambiguity (Business Standard News, 2021 ). Researchers investigating the use of social media for public health during crisis and non-emergency times found that social media serves to improve public health communications by directly reaching the target audience in real time (Berg et al., 2021 ; Fung et al., 2016 ; Li et al., 2021 ; Wong et al., 2017 ). During the COVID-19 pandemic, research assessing the content of public health social media messages has been conducted both internationally and within Canada (Li et al., 2021 ; MacKay et al., 2021 ). Slavik et al. ( 2021 ) conducted a content analysis of Canadian public health authorities’ COVID-19 pandemic Twitter risk communication by geography and found that best practices of health communication were not applied, and differed depending on the severity of COVID-19 in particular regions. MacKay et al. ( 2021 ) studied the crisis communication messaging on Facebook of Canadian public health authorities and news media outlets during the COVID-19 pandemic and found that the guiding principles of crisis messaging were inconsistently applied, particularly trustworthiness, and may have contributed to public negative sentiment. Dubé et al. ( 2022 ) conducted interviews and focus groups of health communication professionals in Québec on their experience during the COVID-19 pandemic and their perception of official communications, using both traditional media and social media; they applied the Crisis and Emergency Risk Communication (CERC, 2018 ) model as their theoretical framework. Similar to MacKay et al. ( 2021 ), the authors found that crisis messaging was not applied consistently for communication interventions, but that messaging about COVID-19 risk level was well received by the Québec public. In sum, while social media has been employed by Canadian public health authorities during the COVID-19 pandemic, there remains work to be done regarding using social media optimally for crisis communications. While this literature is still emerging, there remain gaps on how the technology can be best utilized to address the communication needs of public health and the public during the pandemic. Objectives This retrospective study was part of a larger project that assessed how Canadian public health organizations engaged with the public using social media during the COVID-19 pandemic (Kothari et al., 2021 ). The research question guiding the larger project was: What is the Canadian public health risk communication response to the COVID-19 pandemic in the context of social media? In this current study, we report on what public health leaders and organizations in Canada (provincial, territorial, and federal) and at WHO were communicating on Twitter during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. While this study looks specifically at “what” the Twitter messages communicated, other studies in the larger project assessed the “how” with particular focus on message quality in relation to best practices, such as the CDC’s CERC model and established frameworks for assessing engagement in public health communication. Using sentiment analysis, the final phase of the research assessed how the public responded to public health accounts throughout the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. This research project describes the tweets of public health leaders and organizations across Canada and compares them to tweets from the WHO. The increasing use of social media and the connectivity between the general public and public health leaders present a unique opportunity to assess how digital communications tools were being leveraged during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. In exploring what was communicated (this paper), as well as how it was conveyed and received (quality and engagement assessments and sentiment analysis in forthcoming papers), the project aims to assist the public health community in improving communications strategies to address the COVID-19 pandemic, other public health emergencies, and non-emergency public health issues. Methods A qualitative content analysis was conducted to explore government interventions and announcements during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic, along with corresponding dates via Twitter. The government interventions, such as travel restrictions or physical distances, were identified from a report by the Canadian Institute for Health Information (CIHI, 2022 ). To further understand digital communications in public health, Twitter was selected as the social media channel for this study during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. The first wave in Canada was from the period of January to August 2020 (Detsky & Bogoch, 2021 ). Recent research found an increase in the number of followers for Canadian political and health authority Twitter accounts at the outset of the pandemic as people looked to health professionals for information (Haman, 2021 ). Content analysis is used to assess the presence of concepts and themes within text to further quantify and analyze meanings (Elo & Kyngäs, 2008 ). Twitter posts disseminated by Canadian public health leaders (Chief Medical Officers of Health (CMOH), Chief Public Health Officers (CPHO)), domestic public health organizations, and WHO during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic (January 1–August 31, 2020) were collected. WHO was included as a comparison to Canadian public health accounts as it was assumed the organization would be demonstrating thought (science and policy) and communication leadership throughout the pandemic. As this study is part of a larger project to assess the utilization of crisis communication best practices, it was believed that WHO would closely align as a best practice example. Using government webpages, Google, and Twitter, an environmental scan was done to identify all Canadian governmental public health agencies and authorities, including CMOHs and CPHOs, that were provincial, territorial, or national in scope. Their respective social media addresses and accounts were retrieved as promoted on their webpages during wave 1 of the COVID-19 pandemic. When a province or territory listed more than one public health agency or health organization with a mandate to communicate about the COVID-19 pandemic, all organizations were included in the sample. Québec organizations and leaders were not included as their communications were mainly in French, and it was not feasible to use free or for-purchase translation software programs due to the potential of inaccuracies that could go undetected. In total, 27 Canadian accounts were included for data collection, in addition to WHO’s Twitter account as a source of comparison (Table 1 ), for a total of 28 Twitter accounts for data collection and analysis. Table 1. Data sample—tweets for analysis by account and jurisdiction Name of public health organization or leader Twitter account Number of tweets Region Total World Health Organization WHO 1210 Global 1210 Dr. Bonnie Henry, Provincial Officer of Health, BC DrBonnieHenry 0 BC 357 First Nations Health Authority fnha 52 BC Centre for Disease Control CDCofBC 160 Provincial Health Services Authority of BC PHSAofBC 145 Alberta Ministry of Health GoAHealth 500 AB 798 Dr. Deena Hinshaw, CMOH AB CMOH_Alberta 296 Alberta Health Services AHS_behealthy 2 Ministry of Saskatchewan Health SKGov 200 SK 418 Saskatchewan Health Authority SaskHealth 218 Manitoba Health, Seniors and Active Living MBGov 209 MB 287 Dr. Brent Roussin, CMOH MB roussin_brent 78 Ministry of Health and Long-Term Care—Ontario ONThealth 62 ON 146 Public Health Ontario PublicHealthON 84 Dr. David Williams, CMOH ON TBayMOH 0 Department of Health NB NBHealth 22 NB 22 Health and Community Services NL HCS_GovNL 277 NL 292 Dr. Janice Fitzgerald, CMOH NL CMOH_NL 15 Nova Scotia Health Authority HealthNS 72 NS 306 Department of Health and Wellness—NS nshealth 228 Dr. Robert Strang, CMOH NS StrangRobert 6 Health PEI Health_PEI 104 PE 104 Dr. Kami Kandola, CPHO NWT NWT_CPHO 127 NT 130 Northwest Territories Health and Social Services Authority NTHSSA 3 Department of Health and Social Services—YK HSSYukon 35 YT 35 Department of Health and Social Services—NU GOVofNUNAVUT 26 NU 26 Public Health Agency of Canada GovCanHealth 514 CA 1055 Dr. Teresa Tam—Chief Public Health Officer CA CPHO_Canada 541 Total 5186 Open in a new tab Key policy interventions mentioned in a CIHI report (Canadian Institute for Health Information, 2022 ) were selected by the researchers and then used as markers to gather a smaller sample of tweets for analysis. The CIHI COVID-19 Intervention Scan is a continuously updated database and timeline of all federal, provincial, and territorial government policy interventions in Canada. It has been applied in other COVID-19 pandemic research (Keshteli et al., 2021 ; Polisena et al., 2021 ). This database includes announcements and other public health measures aimed at managing and mitigating the COVID-19 pandemic (Canadian Institute for Health Information, 2022 ). It would be too cumbersome and less impactful to assess all tweets throughout wave 1 of the pandemic. Instead, using the CIHI COVID-19 Intervention Scan, the researchers selected key interventions and announcements from each jurisdiction (federal, provincial, territorial) to serve as starting points for data collection. By focusing on key interventions instead of all the tweets, we felt we would capture the most important and pivotal communications on Twitter. We deemed government interventions and announcements as “key” or important if they impacted a significant proportion of the population, e.g., the opening and closure of schools. From the selected key interventions and their corresponding dates as indicated by the CIHI Intervention Scan, we decided to expand the timeline to 3 days pre- and post-intervention. This created a specific timeline for collecting tweets in each jurisdiction that were considered key to the pandemic trajectory and yielded a sample of selective tweets. We chose 3 days before and after an intervention was launched because it would capture relevant communication leading up to an intervention announcement along with any additional clarifying messages which may be disseminated shortly after an intervention. Because of the diverse trajectory of the pandemic, including differences in regional disease positivity rates, community transmission, and government policy responses, the policy interventions and data collection timelines selected were unique to each jurisdiction. The same process was used to determine data collection from the WHO account; however, key interventions were determined using WHO’s COVID-19 Response Timeline (World Health Organization, 2020 ). Key policy interventions between WHO and the Canadian jurisdictions did not align as the context and timeline of the pandemic were different in Canada and globally. Data collection Tweets from the 28 public health accounts (27 Canadian and 1 WHO) were collected using a combination of Twitter’s standard and premium application programming interfaces (APIs) and the Python (van Rossum, 2009 ) libraries tweepy and searchtweets (Pigott et al., 2020 ; Roesslein, ( n.d. )). Data collection began in July 2020 and continued through October 2020. Only true “tweets” were captured as outgoing messages to the public. Replies to threads were not included as they were deemed not to be based off original public health–created discussions. This resulted in 40,623 tweets from the public health accounts posted from January 1 to September 3, 2020, the wave 1 timeline. Because this was a very large sample of tweets, the research team decided to instead target key points in time and the related public health interventions to fine-tune our sample. In applying our parameters to focus on key policy dates, 25,679 tweets were selected for the initial data collection sample using the 3-day pre- and post-intervention timeline as described above. See Fig. 1 for a flow chart explaining the data collection process. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Study flow of tweet sampling process Because the sample included all tweets from public health accounts, we then identified tweets related to the COVID-19 pandemic. This was achieved programmatically using a list of keywords and phrases (Appendix 1 ) which was expanded after evaluating the remaining tweets to assess whether there were other appropriate keywords and phrases. A total of 7197 tweets were excluded using a Python process because they were deemed not related to COVID-19. The public health tweets were then sorted into “threads” so that only tweets from a given public health account that either were not replies to another tweet or were replying only to a tweet from that same public health account which was posted during a 3-day pre- and post-intervention period were included. A total of 3077 tweets were removed in this step. This left 15,405 tweets, which was deemed to be too large for manual coding. In order to create a more manageable sample, for accounts with 200 or more tweets we included a randomly selected sample of 35% during the January 1–August 31, 2020, timeframe, using the Python library pandas (McKinney, 2010 ). A 35% subset was thought to provide a representative sample as this approach has been used in other social media research (McKinney, 2010 ). With this process, 9778 tweets were removed across 10 accounts. Tweets were de-identified so that only Twitter handles belonging to government organizations, non-profit organizations, and public figures remained. This left 5627 tweets that were reviewed by the research team. Next, a series of tweets were removed by the researchers because of language other than English and lack of relevance to the COVID-19 pandemic. This left a final sample of 5186 for analysis. Data analysis A content analysis of tweets was conducted using a framework developed from the integration of the CIHI COVID-19 Intervention Scan and the Wong et al. ( 2017 ) social media coding framework (Canadian Institute for Health Information, 2022 ; Wong et al., 2017 ). The CIHI COVID-19 Intervention Scan is an online database maintained by CIHI to track and categorize Canadian policy and intervention responses to the COVID-19 pandemic by all provinces, territories, and the federal government. The resource groups interventions and announcements into 10 categories: case management, closures and openings, distancing, health services, health workforce, public information, state of emergency, travel, financial and economic, and vaccine (Canadian Institute for Health Information, 2022 ). These categories, their definitions, and sub-measures formed the basis of our content analysis coding framework. Detailed descriptions of these intervention categories and sub-measures are provided in Table 2 . Table 2. CIHI policy intervention categories and sub-measures with the adapted Wong et al. ( 2017 ) framework Intervention category Definitions and sub-measures Case management • Assessment centres information • Case definition • Self-isolation • Education • Prevention* • Dispelling myths* • Contact tracing • Online assessment tools • Test criteria • Outbreak definitions • Transmission • Risks and symptoms • Epidemiological findings* Openings and closings • Recreation • Education • Daycare • Non-essential services • Health services Physical distancing • Restrictions on public places • Restrictions on social gatherings • Work from home (recommendations and directives) • Other (curfews) • Physical distancing and social gathering advice Health workforce capacity • Change in practice • Licence reinstatement/reclassification • Safety guidelines • Supply management • Highlighting workforce • Capacity/strength* Health services • Delayed medical procedures • Equipment • Resumed medical procedures • Telemedicine/virtual care • Visitors • Other Travel • Travel restrictions • Travel self-isolation • Travel advice Vaccine • Regulatory approval • Purchasing and allocation • Population to be vaccinated • Phase changes and milestones • Implementation plans and frameworks Public information • Mask advice • Mask mandate • Resources** (all promotion, not just initial launch) • Projections and modelling • Pandemic response plans/preparedness (education, local, P/T, fed gov, non-profit, pro-profit, hospital, foreign) • Phase and alert level changes Financial and economic • Research and development funding • Business funding • Income support State of emergency • Declaration of pandemic* • Public health emergency* • State of emergency News article* • Sharing of media pieces Event promotion* • Speech or media conference • Webchat • Other Mental health*** • Psycho-social well-being • Substance abuse • Domestic violence Gratitude*** • Praise for frontline workers • Health care professionals for their services • Public for compliance with public health measures • Countries, governments and businesses for adopting public health measures or providing financial assistance to combat the burden of COVID-19 Other • Facilitating community solidarity • Addressing climate change • COVID-19 economic recovery • Tips for healthy living (physical activity and eating) during the COVID-19 pandemic Open in a new tab *Wong supplement **Expansion of definition ***Inductive coding Wong et al. ( 2017 ) analyzed content on Twitter related to the 2014 Ebola outbreak as communicated by local US health departments. The Wong et al. ( 2017 ) framework was developed through inductive content analysis and categorized tweets into 4 categories: information giving, news update, event promotion, and preparedness. The framework was developed for pandemic-specific crisis communications and has been applied in numerous settings (e.g., e-cigarette and diabetes public health campaigns on Twitter) and research inquiries (Harris et al., 2013 , 2014 ). The CIHI Intervention Scan was created to specifically categorize the Canadian response to the COVID-19 pandemic. Merging the two frameworks provided a trusted and flexible method to conduct the content analysis. Two unique categories not found within the CIHI Scan were adapted from the Wong et al. ( 2017 ) framework and added to our coding framework—news article and event promotion. Before analysis began, the case management category was further expanded beyond the concise CIHI definition to include aspects of Wong et al.’s ( 2017 ) description including dispelling myths, prevention, and epidemiological findings. These two categories from Wong et al. ( 2017 ) supplemented the CIHI Policy Intervention Scan to create our coding framework (see Table 2 for detailed descriptions). A codebook was developed by three team members and piloted by two members to assess applicability. Changes to the codebook were proposed to the entire research team before codebook revisions and application to the larger dataset. All content analysis coding was done using NVivo 12 (QSR International, 2020 ). A single tweet could be coded to multiple categories. Any codes that did not fit within the pre-established frameworks were developed into a new category by the first author and a trained research assistant. At the outset, the coding sub-team had multiple discussions with the entire research team to clarify coding ambiguities. Given that Twitter data were available in the public domain, no institutional ethics approval was required. Results A total of 5186 tweets were included in our analysis: 1210 from WHO and 3976 from Canadian public health organization and leader accounts. Table 2 depicts the number of tweets by account and by jurisdiction. Two public health leader accounts (Dr. Bonnie Henry, CMOH BC; and Dr. David Williams, CMOH ON) contained no tweets within the data collection range or at any point in the first wave. Fourteen percent ( n = 685) of the tweets did not fit into the pre-determined policy intervention categories. Three additional non-intervention categories (mental health, gratitude, and other) emerged during the content analysis and were added to the coding framework. Overall, there were 15 categories for coding tweets, 12 of which came from existing frameworks and three of which were added through inductive exploration. On average, each jurisdiction contained 11.86 different policy intervention categories within their tweets (Table 3 ). In assessing breadth of the COVID-19 pandemic tweets, BC accounts communicated with the greatest range of COVID-19-related tweets with content across all 15 categories. Less populated provinces/territories (NU, YK, PEI) had fewer categories represented in their tweets. Nunavut Twitter accounts featured the smallest range with four categories represented in their tweets including case management, travel restrictions, public information, and event promotion. Table 3. Tweet category range by jurisdiction Jurisdiction CA AB BC SK MB ON NB NL NS PE YT NU NT WHO Avg Number of categories featured in sample 14 14 15 14 12 12 9 14 13 9 9 4 13 14 11.86 Open in a new tab Table 4 depicts the frequency of categories across the sample and by jurisdiction. The most commonly found category throughout the sample was public information, which was seen in 44.8% ( n = 2683) of all tweets. As shown in the example (Fig. 2 ), tweets in this category often included links to government resources for more information about the pandemic and/or announcements about changes to the phased approach of public health measures. The latter was particularly common in provinces where cities or regions were on different COVID-19 pandemic trajectories and shelter-in-place orders and phases ranged across the province. Table 4. Proportion of tweets in each category by jurisdiction Public information Case finding and management Physical distancing Event promotion Gratitude Health services Mental health Travel restrictions and other measures Other Health workforce capacity Financial and economic measures Openings and closures Vaccine State of emergency News article Jurisdiction CA 42.7 32.3 9.7 5 3.2 1.4 5.1 5.8 2.6 0.9 1.8 0.1 0.4 0.4 0 AB 36.5 40.9 11 8.6 4.1 4.4 5.4 2.3 4.8 2.1 2.5 2.4 0.4 0.3 0 BC 52.7 47.9 4.8 7 3.1 9.5 5.6 8.4 2.5 6.4 1.1 1.7 0.3 1.1 1.1 SK 54.8 56.5 12 10 3.1 4.3 1.9 5.7 3.3 1.7 2.4 1.9 1 0.7 0 MB 57.8 39 10.8 17.8 2.4 2.4 3.8 5.2 5.6 1 4.9 1.4 0 0 0 ON 75.3 15.8 11 0 4.1 8.2 8.2 2.7 0.7 6.2 4.8 3.4 0 0.7 0 NB 27.3 90.9 13.6 4.5 0 9.1 4.5 4.5 0 4.5 0 0 4.5 0 0 NL 42.5 32.2 19.5 13.4 3.8 2.1 9.2 7.5 2.1 3.1 3.4 4.5 1.4 0.3 0 NS 37.6 58.2 5.6 9.2 2.3 4.9 1 3.6 2 6.9 2 1.6 0 0 2 PE 15.4 19.2 4.8 5.8 1 18.3 5.8 