 also included celebration days like “nurses’ week” or World Health Day. These often coincided with the gratitude category (4.6%) where healthcare professionals were praised and thanked for their service and support. Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Tweet from Chief Public Health Officer of Canada Tweets about health services (4.4%) and gratitude (4.6%) were less common throughout the sample. Health services tweets promoted virtual care, introduced changes to health care service delivery (as shown in Fig. 7 ), or reaffirmed the public to continue the use of emergency services for acute care issues such as stroke. Gratitude was expressed in tweets for front line workers for their support and communities for adopting and complying with public health measures. Tweets containing messaging about travel restrictions ( n = 215), mental health ( n = 231), and other ( n = 207) were found across approximately 4% of the tweets. Travel restrictions included information about government advisories to avoid non-essential travel (Fig. 8 ), mandatory quarantine post travel, and closure of regional borders. Fig. 7. Open in a new tab Tweet from Health Prince Edward Island Fig. 8. Open in a new tab Tweet from Northwest Territories Chief Public Health Officer Tweets about health workforce capacity ( n = 159), financial and economic measures ( n = 150), openings and closures ( n = 75), COVID-19 vaccines ( n = 58), states of emergency ( n = 32), or sharing of new articles ( n = 10) were least common making up less than 3% of the sample. Health workforce capacity tweets featured calls for retired professionals to help with the overburdened health care system (Fig. 9 ) and also showcased the current professionals working in the field. Financial and economic measures most often highlighted new monetary supports for individuals and businesses to overcome financial hardship during the pandemic (Fig. 10 ) or showcased funding campaigns aimed to support countries in managing the COVID-19 pandemic through research and service delivery. As shown in Fig. 11 , within the opening and closing category, tweets often described which businesses or services would be affected by the tightening or loosening of policies. Fig. 9. Open in a new tab Tweet from Health Prince Edward Island Fig. 10. Open in a new tab Tweet from Manitoba Government Fig. 11. Open in a new tab Tweet from Government of Saskatchewan Tweet content by jurisdiction There were differences between jurisdictions when comparing content frequencies at provincial, territorial, federal, and international levels (Table 4 ). Two comparisons are employed in this section: (1) Canada as a whole compared to WHO, and (2) Canadian jurisdictions compared to one another. In assessing the tweet content between the WHO and the Canadian accounts, both tweeted about public information most often. While case findings and management tweets were the next most common content, this category only comprised 26.6% of the WHO sample versus 40.92% of the Canadian sample. WHO was also less likely to tweet about physical distancing (2.8% of sample), travel restrictions (0.5% of sample), or event promotion (5.9%) compared to Canadian accounts (10.5%, 5.1%, and 8.2% respectively). WHO tweets did contain a higher frequency of tweets with the gratitude theme (9.3% vs 3.2%) and information about the COVID-19 vaccine (2.1% of sample vs 0.5% across Canadian accounts) (Figs. 12 and 13 ). Fig. 12. Open in a new tab Tweet from the World Health Organization Fig. 13. Open in a new tab Tweet from the World Health Organization Across Canadian jurisdictions, there were regional differences in tweet content. The federal accounts aligned closely with the frequencies seen across the rest of the Canadian sample. Alberta tweets were also similar in composition across categories to the rest of the Canadian regions with the exception that case management and findings were most frequent over public information. These two categories were the inverse for most of the Canadian sample. Much of the Alberta sample (40.9%) was comprised of tweets from the CMOH providing updates on epidemiological findings (Fig. 14 ). Fig. 14. Open in a new tab Tweet from the Alberta Chief Medical Officer of Health Most tweets from British Columbia public health accounts were of public information, case findings/management, travel restrictions, and health care in comparison with other provinces and territories. Tweets from British Columbia were less likely to include content related to physical distancing compared to the rest of the Canadian accounts (4.8% vs. 10.5%). Saskatchewan tweets were focused on public information (54.8%) or case findings and management (56.5%) with minimal content around mental health in comparison with other jurisdictions (1.9% vs 4.8%). Tweets from Ontario and Manitoba public health accounts were heavily weighted towards public information as compared with other regions. In fact, more than 75% of tweets from Ontario accounts were categorized as public information. As shown in Fig. 15 , an example of a tweet classified as public information, Public Health ON tweets often lacked details about case management/findings or public health interventions, but instead referred to a weblink for further information. Yukon, Ontario, and Manitoba were also more likely than other jurisdictions to tweet about financial and economic measures, making up 8.6%, 4.9%, and 4.8% of their samples respectively versus 2.3% average across all Canadian jurisdictions. The New Brunswick sample of tweets was almost entirely dominated (90.9%) by case finding/management-related content. Physical distancing, travel restrictions, and mental health tweets appeared more frequently in Newfoundland and Labrador tweets than in those of other Canadian regions (19.5% vs 10.5%, 7.5% vs 5.1%, and 9.2% vs 4.8%, respectively). The majority of tweets from Nova Scotia public health accounts focused on case finding and management (58.2%) as opposed to public information which was most common in the other regions. The most common category of tweets among PEI public health accounts was health workforce capacity (42.3%), where the government asked former health care professionals for assistance with staffing issues (Fig. 9 ). Tweets from public health in the Yukon most frequently included content around case findings/management, physical distancing, or openings/closures. The Nunavut tweets were heavily weighted towards case findings/management (84.6%) as were those of the Northwest Territories (53.8%). Fig. 15. Open in a new tab Tweet from Public Health Ontario Discussion This study demonstrates that public health organizations and leaders are engaging with Twitter to share information related to the COVID-19 pandemic. Findings from this study showcase what public health leaders and organizations communicated to individuals accessing the social media platform Twitter, and the analysis of their messages adds to our understanding of how public health communicated during the pandemic. The content analysis is the first step in categorizing and charting communications and enables the comparison and assessment of risk communications strategies now and going forward. Among public health leaders within Canada, most (7 out of 12) CMOH were not active on Twitter during the first wave of the pandemic. Five regional leaders (SK, PEI, NU, YK, and NB) did not have a Twitter account. Two leaders (BC and ON) were present on Twitter but did not post any content during the period studied. As prior research shows, public health leaders are uniquely positioned to lead and be the face of communication strategies during public health emergencies as a result of their expertise and credibility with the public, and social media is an effective means to reach large populations in real time (Jin et al., 2019 ). Leaders from BC and ON, two of the most populous provinces, were not active on Twitter where COVID-19 rates were some of the highest in Canada during the first wave. This result highlights a potential lost opportunity for public health leaders to reach the substantial number of individuals using Twitter as a public health information resource and to increase transparency and trust in public health officials. It should be noted that while BC did not have a public health leader (i.e., MOH) active on Twitter, the province did have the most active Twitter accounts for public health organizations ( n = 3) compared to the rest of the country. In addition, other assessments of BC’s health communications during the pandemic evaluated its public health leader and the accompanying communications strategy to be strong and effective at keeping COVID-19 rates low in the province (Tworek et al., 2020 ). Overall, tweets from public health leaders and organizations throughout the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic were oriented around public information and case management and findings. This finding aligns with the results from other studies, including Wong et al. ( 2017 ), and is to be expected during a first wave of a pandemic, where one aim of public health is to impart accurate and timely information. While specific public health interventions, such as physical distancing, travel restrictions, and closing businesses, were being adopted by governments throughout the first wave, there was less focus on communicating about these specific interventions, and more posts focused on information sharing. By not communicating specific interventions, public health authorities may have missed an opportunity to prime the public and build further awareness of public health policy measures. Similar findings were reported in the literature (Dubé et al., 2022 ; MacKay et al., 2021 ; Slavik et al., 2021 ; Tang et al., 2021 ). For example, based on their similar finding of straightforward information communication, Dubé et al. ( 2022 ) suggest that conveying information without explaining the rationale behind preventive behaviours disempowers the public. The WHO Twitter sample was added to the dataset with the aim of providing a comparison and charting how an international thought leader on science and policy influenced the policy response and communication coming out of Canadian regions. Instead, our research found that WHO communicated in different message categories. In comparing WHO to the Canadian sample, WHO posts were more likely to contain public information–related organizational and governmental preparedness plans. Many of the WHO tweets addressed how WHO was responding to the pandemic, perhaps as a result of criticisms WHO faced during the first wave. Some countries and media questioned whether WHO acted fast enough or accurately assessed the threat of COVID-19 in the early days (Wood, 2020 ). Furthermore, the President of the United States at the time threatened to defund WHO as a result of pandemic mismanagement (Wood, 2020 ). WHO’s Twitter efforts around promoting their preparedness plans may have been in response to such perceived threats. Unfortunately, a heavy focus on explaining organizational plans (as seen in a large proportion of WHO tweets describing organizational plans) may have hindered WHO’s use of the Twitter platform at a time when the public wanted accurate and speedy health information. Furthermore, WHO was less likely than Canadian accounts to communicate about travel restrictions or advise the public to avoid travel, aligning with the public stance WHO conveyed about stepping back from travel restrictions (World Health Organization, 2020 ). A social media pandemic communication model (Lim et al., 2017 ), which incorporates WHO’s Pandemic Influenza Phase Continuum, notes that early pandemic phases require giving physical instructions to individuals and information to reduce citizen concerns, which was done by Canada. In contrast, the WHO tended to tweet information typically meant for the tail end of a pandemic, such as maintaining an organization’s reputation. There were major differences in the nature of tweets among Canadian public health leaders. The territories heavily oriented their Twitter communications about the pandemic towards case findings/management which often highlighted that COVID-19 rates were low in those regions. This type of communication is expected when community transmission remains low and only baseline public health measures are being implemented in the region. On the other hand, in Ontario, where community transmission was high, Twitter communications were focused on directing the public to alternate resources for information, instead of specifying epidemiological outcomes. This lack of content on specific epidemiological outcomes may constitute a missed opportunity for public health leaders to maintain transparency in a region with high community transmission. Anchoring public health messages with details of the desired behaviour or outcomes may improve communication across varied groups (National Cancer Institute, 2011 ). In the future, Ontario accounts should consider leveraging specifics to better contextualize their message. Evaluations performed on the overall Ontario pandemic communications strategy reported a lack of transparency (Tworek et al., 2020 ). At the same time, public health leaders need to be aware of the instability of Twitter. A recent example is the effect of former Twitter CEO Elon Musk’s takeover of the platform, leading to a variety of technical and operational issues including removing bans on accounts promoting misinformation, changes in subscription formats, and widespread outages (O’Sullivan & Duffy, 2022 ; Mac et al., 2023 ). Regional variation in Twitter content can also be related to socio-economic and cultural contexts within a jurisdiction. The territories were more likely to include content about physical distancing. While population density is sparse in these areas, overcrowded living conditions could lead to outbreaks and may have been a concern among public health professionals. Coastal province (BC, PEI, and NL) tweets contained a higher proportion of travel restrictions and advice. An emphasis on travel restrictions communication also aligns with the establishment of the Atlantic bubble—an effective policy that required all outsiders to quarantine upon entry to Atlantic Canada and delayed community transmission during the first wave. Further inductive coding beyond the CIHI Intervention Scan and the Wong et al. ( 2017 ) framework demonstrated mental health and gratitude as strong themes within COVID-19 pandemic tweets. While these themes were not policy or intervention categories, mental health complications are a by-product of the pandemic and warrant further analysis in forthcoming studies within this project. This result is corroborated by other recent social media studies that found mental health among prominent themes during the COVID-19 pandemic, and that social media data may be useful for surveillance (Tsao et al., 2021 ). Gratitude is a crisis communications best practice approach that is important to capture because it sets a tone around message delivery (CERC, 2018 ). Additional research can assess how gratitude was layered with certain intervention categories or used as a separate message throughout the pandemic. Each of these themes will be further explored in future studies within this project which look at the utilization of crisis communication best practices across the sample and the public’s response to COVID-19 pandemic tweets through sentiment analysis. Our findings need to be considered in the context of study strengths and limitations. To ensure the trustworthiness of the analysis and our interpretations, our team used varying perspectives to query the data and findings. Team members range from junior to senior level researchers; furthermore, members bring expertise in computer science, social media platforms, public health, digital health, and health communications. An audit trail was created through memoing and team meeting minutes. Finally, we shared preliminary findings and received feedback from peers at a public health academic conference. We used the Twitter API for Academic Research to identify tweets to collect, which allowed us to have access to the full dataset of tweets on Twitter with no gaps or missing tweets. However, if published tweets were deleted or removed pre-data collection, they would not have been available to collect using the Twitter API. Using the CIHI COVID-19 Intervention Scan, we systematically categorized tweet content to compare communication responses. While the existing definitions for each intervention category were at times restrictive, the large sample size provided a good communication snapshot. A more inductive approach might have led to different classifications or themes but then comparisons with other studies would be challenging. For feasibility, we randomly selected tweets from our 3-day pre- and post-intervention time period, but we did not stratify nor proportionally weigh the sampling procedure. We used particular keywords to identify relevant tweets, but it is possible that some tweets without those words were missed. We did not translate and analyze French tweets, as doing so might have led to a unique understanding of public health communication among French-speaking individuals. This study examined Twitter, which is a leading social media data source for research but only spotlights one aspect of a comprehensive risk communication strategy. The pandemic is a dynamic event, and Twitter communications in subsequent COVID-19 pandemic waves are likely different than what is reported here. Future research is needed to establish what type of social media messaging leads to protective behaviours in public health emergencies. Conclusion By exploring the Twitter content of public health organizations and leaders, this research uncovered missed opportunities both in terms of social media participation by leaders, and also related to the types of messages that were communicated. Public information was the most common type of Twitter post across public health accounts. But an emphasis by some regions on “public information” which was geared towards promoting organizational plans and sharing resources, instead of providing details within Twitter posts, may have limited the impact and transparency of communications during the pandemic. While this research only explored what was said, it provides a useful snapshot of how public health made use of Twitter during the first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. Because Twitter communications were shown to vary across population and jurisdictional characteristics, leaders and organizations can look to established principles and then develop competencies to guide their efforts. In addition, public health officials using Twitter should remain cautious of the “fragility” of the medium, and how it may impact their health messaging via social media platforms. Future research is needed to establish whether and how social media messaging leads to the desired behaviour change in emergency situations. Contributions to knowledge What does this study add to existing knowledge? This study adds to our understanding of how public health organizations and leaders in Canadian provinces and territories and at the international level engaged with Twitter, a popular social media platform, during the first wave of the pandemic. To our knowledge, it is the first study to provide a snapshot of social media use by public health during the COVID-19 pandemic and contributes to our understanding of this important innovation in the public health setting. The content analysis enables the comparison and assessment of risk communications strategies going forward. What are the key implications for public health interventions, practice, or policy? Health communication is an essential competency in public health practice, and this research sets a baseline to strengthen this skillset. The categorization of tweets enables a general assessment of what intervention areas public health prioritized and minimized in communications strategies. This research informs public health about the strengths and weaknesses in their existing digital communication tactics. Understanding these areas will serve public health well as the community continues to engage with risk communications for the COVID-19 pandemic and future pandemics. Future research can focus on identifying essential social media competencies, and ultimately determining the association between social media communication and behaviour change among population segments. Appendix 1 Search terms Word or phrase 1 2 m'' 2 2 m '' 3 2 meters away'' 4 2 metre'' 5 2 metres away'' 6 abtracetogether'' 7 albertarelaunch'' 8 all in this together'' 9 aparttogether'' 10 aplanirlacourbe'' 11 aplanissonslacourbe'' 12 asymptomatic'' 13 at least 2 metres'' 14 autoisolement'' 15 autoisolezvous'' 16 bubble'' 17 cerb'' 18 chinavirus'' 19 chloroquine'' 20 closecanadianborders'' 21 closetheborder'' 22 closetheschools'' 23 cohort group'' 24 cohorting'' 25 community spread'' 26 community transmission'' 27 confirmed or suspected case'' 28 contacttracing'' 29 corona virus'' 30 corona'' 31 coronavirus'' 32 covd19'' 33 covetiquette'' 34 covi19'' 35 covid_tag_roots_infection = [ ''reducethespread'' 36 covid'' 37 creative way to connect'' 38 creative ways families are connecting'' 39 creative ways families connect'' 40 creative ways friends are connecting'' 41 creative ways friends connect'' 42 creative ways to connect with family'' 43 creative ways to connect with friends'' 44 creative ways to connect'' 45 creative ways you connect with family'' 46 creative ways you connect with friends'' 47 déconfinement'' 48 disinfect'' 49 distanciationphysique'' 50 distanciationsociale'' 51 distancing'' 52 dontbeaspreader'' 53 drive-in services'' 54 éloignementphysique'' 55 extraordinary situation'' 56 extraordinary times'' 57 flatten the curv