INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7135,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12505715','EURO-MUSCULUS/USPRM phantom recipe for (musculoskeletal) interventional ultrasound training.','Salce G, Jačisko J, Ricci V, Mezian K, Özçakar L.','2025','cheese musculus usprm paralleling herewith textures beginners edible granulated kettle hob screws coins batteries grapes tomatoes dyed balloons pens pencils psyllium mucilloid coloring tablespoons coloring obliging psyllium tablespoons refrigerated needless solidification tomato pens supermarkets edible granulated pork cyanoacrylate glue kettle hob psyllium mucilloid coloring tablespoons granulated ballistic coloring psyllium mucilloid pouring fluidity container coloring solidified periosteum echoic spinous convex envelop convexity layering convex pou container psyllium solidified refrigeration overturned firmness poured voids melting refrigeration container cyanoacrylate glue pork fibrocartilaginous knives scalpels impregnated psyllium superficially dermo glenohumeral labrum pork paratenon solidified humeral glenoid labrum infraspinatus deltoid glenohumeral paratenon hydro erector spinae spinous erector spinae pork pork enveloped paratenon solidified hydro epitenon paratenon hydro epitenon paratenon entrapped tilting homemade pork beef homemade giannotti reused melting detaching fleshy dishwasher reprocessed paralleling herewith cookbook customize supplementary_digital_material1 supplementary_digital_material2 supplementary_digital_material2 supplementary_digital_material3 supplementary_digital_material4 supplementary_digital_material5 26mb 25mb ozcakar mezian ultrason orlandi corazza arcidiacono serafini sconfienza berh whaley urman s11916 abdulsalam ozcakar dummies staniek s00540 sutcliffe hardman dornbluth kist iohom jmu bude jcu ramnarine hoskins s0301 charnoff naqvi homemade e116 ackley solari homemade giannotti jethwa closs bhatti homemade gervasoni giulio fari andreoli s40477 bellingham 0b013e3181c75a76 groves keita talla kikinis fichtinger mousavi tbme pme zach tewari tito s13089 giannotti jethwa closs bhatti homemade jacisko mezian guvener kobesova ozcakar mnemonics metaphorical sonoanatomy e189 jacisko mezian guvener mnemonics metaphorical sonopathologies supplementary_digital_material1 supplementary_digital_material2 supplementary_digital_material2 supplementary_digital_material3 supplementary_digital_material4 supplementary_digital_material5 26mb 25mb','Abstract Considering the growing role of ultrasound-guided procedures in musculoskeletal medicine, training as regards these interventions is pivotal. While hands-on training on cadavers can be considered optimal, it has several drawbacks, e.g. , high cost, poor availability, and technical challenges regarding preservation. Apart from cadavers, different approaches to practicing needle guidance are taught in ultrasound workshops whereby phantoms from meat ( e.g. , chicken breast), cheese or gelatin are used. Likewise, this article aims to provide a detailed description as to how different gelatin-based phantoms can be prepared. In line with the EURO-MUSCULUS/USPRM (European Musculoskeletal Ultrasound Study Group/Ultrasound Study Group of the International Society of Physical and Rehabilitation Medicine) protocols/background, the authors describe particular basic and advanced phantoms to be used for practicing different technical/manual skills pertaining to common ultrasound-guided procedures. The present manuscript can be considered a practical and ready-to-use “recipe book” for readers who are interested in the wide spectrum of interventional ultrasound. K ey words : Musculoskeletal system, Ultrasonography, Computer-assisted surgery, Injections, Imaging phantoms Paralleling the role of ultrasound (US) examination in musculoskeletal medicine, US-guided (US-G) procedures are increasingly used in everyday clinical practice. 1 - 6 Herewith/technically, they require a long learning curve, and the outcome (accuracy as well as efficacy) inevitably depends on the competency of the individual performing the procedure. Notably, it is not uncommon to deal with different challenges during the procedures, e.g. , choosing the wrong needle length/gauge, losing the needle in the screen, inserting the needle at an incorrect site, or with a wrong angle. In this sense, the physician needs to practice different skills before he/she can start doing such delicate injections in real patients. 7 , 8 Needle-transducer alignment, hand-eye coordination, and identification of the most suitable injection site are common/exemplary technical pitfalls ( Table I ), especially for novice physicians. 6 Table I. — Pitfalls in musculoskeletal USG procedures. Skill Technical note Needle-probe alignment The practitioner must keep the needle as parallel as possible with the US beam during the entire intervention i.e. to optimize its real-time visualization during the in-plane technique Hand-eye coordination While advancing the needle within the patient’s body, the practitioner must observe its “digital version” on the screen i.e. not the “physical version” attached to the syringe Needle entry planning The practitioner must plan the optimal distance/angle between the transducer’s edge and the needle entry point to accurately advance the needle’s tip towards the anatomical target Long needle handling The practitioner must manipulate a long needle inside the tissues avoiding specific anatomical elements ( e.g. , vessels, nerves) and overcoming the “fear” of reaching deep/distant targets Open in a new tab US: ultrasound. Animal tissues and gel-based phantoms are the most commonly used tools to guarantee specific training in several US-G interventions, such as biopsies and peripheral nerve blocks. 7 , 9 , 10 The first phantoms used in the 1980s were simple i.e. of either gelatin or agar. 11 Later, different textures and materials were used to make the phantoms more similar to the human tissue – providing realistic sonographic feedback of the procedure. 8 , 12 , 13 Nowadays, accurate reproduction of human anatomy and histology can be provided in phantoms, at a reasonable cost as well. Accordingly, this paper primarily aims to describe how different basic and advanced phantoms can be prepared for practicing various US-G musculoskeletal procedures. By facilitating such training, the secondary aim would be to encourage/support less experienced clinicians to perform prompt US-G interventions. Materials and methods For better clarity and comprehensiveness, the phantoms are categorized as basic and advanced. While the former (Supplementary Digital Material 1: Supplementary Figure 1) can be used to train basic manual skills such as needle visualization on the screen, the latter can also simulate certain anatomical structures and provide higher training experience. 10 , 14 , 15 Basic phantoms Needle visualization using in-plane and out-of-plane techniques, manual control or alignment of the transducer/needle and hand-eye coordination are some of the skills that can be trained using phantoms for beginners – i.e. , basic principles of US-G interventions. 6 , 10 , 15 All the suggested items to prepare basic phantoms are listed in Table II . Table II. — Recipe for the basic phantoms. Edible granulated gelatin (40 grams per liter of water) Variable shape plastic containers Hot water 80 °C (can be heated with a kettle, microwave, or cooking hob) Spatial exploration (hyperechoic recognizable objects such as screws, coins, batteries, etc.) Needle visualization/injection (grapes or cherry tomatoes) Aspiration (dyed water balloons, pens, pencils, etc.) Psyllium hydrophilic mucilloid fibers * (Supplementary Figure 1D) Food coloring (1-2 grams per liter of water) # Open in a new tab *Optional, to modulate the echogenicity of the gelatin (one to three tablespoons per liter of water); # optional, depending on the degree of transparency necessary for the training session. Preparation The gelatin mixture represents the “matrix” of the basic phantom. Different types of food coloring can be used to make the internal targets invisible to human eye from outside – obliging their visibility only by US. Likewise, different concentrations of psyllium fibers can be added to adjust the overall echogenicity of the basic phantom, ranging from one to three tablespoons per liter of water. 14 The phantom is then allowed to cool at room temperature, typically for about 30 minutes, before being refrigerated for a minimum of 12 hours. Needless to say, this timing is critical for the gelatin solidification and the phantom’s structural integrity. Application For spatial exploration (Supplementary Digital Material 2: Supplementary Figure 2, 3), the physician should initially/thoroughly scan the opaque phantom to locate and identify the objects. Needle visualization ( Figure 1 ) can be practiced with several attempts using the in-plane and out-of-plane techniques. Figure 1. Open in a new tab —Phantom for visualizing the needle as well as for practicing in-plane and out-of-plane techniques. Injection (Supplementary Digital Material 3: Supplementary Figure 4) can be performed with a bolus of water/gel inside/adjacent to a specific target e.g. , tomato or grape hidden in the gelatin. Aspiration (Supplementary Digital Material 4: Supplementary Figure 5) can be performed from a specific hollow object e.g. , a balloon filled with red dye or gel deposit inside a phantom. Additional objects can be positioned along the needle’s pathway (such as pens) to increase the technical difficulty of the US-G procedure. Again, different syringes can be used for injection and aspiration – simulating the real-life scenarios and challenging the motor tasks alike. Advanced phantoms Exemplary materials and objects – easily available on common online platforms or supermarkets (Supplementary Digital Material 5: Supplementary Figure 6) – for preparing the advanced phantoms are listed in Table III . Table III. — Recipe for the advanced phantoms. Edible granulated gelatin (40 grams per liter of water) § (Supplementary Figure 1A) Plastic anatomical models (Supplementary Image 1B) Chicken breast, pork tendon, or any other animal tissue (Supplementary Figure 1C) Variable shape plastic containers Cyanoacrylate glue Hot water 80 °C (can be heated with a kettle, microwave, or cooking hob) Psyllium hydrophilic mucilloid fibers * (Supplementary Figure 1D) Food coloring (1-2 grams per liter of water) # Open in a new tab § 70-80 grams per liter of water if a higher consistency is necessary for the training session ( e.g. , needling procedure); * optional, to modulate the echogenicity of the gelatin (one to three tablespoons per liter of water); # optional, depending on the degree of transparency necessary for the training session. Preparation Using 250-300 bloom granulated gelatin is suggested to obtain a matrix with firm consistency, akin to ballistic gel. Again, food coloring can be added to the gelatin to hide the target objects and psyllium hydrophilic mucilloid fibers to obtain a sonographic texture similar to real human subcutaneous tissue. 15 The mixture is dissolved in hot water (80 °C) and then cooled to approximately 40 °C before pouring. The hot mixture might otherwise cause thermal damage to the preparation. Sufficient fluidity should be retained to infiltrate all the spaces – avoiding air formation as well. Plastic model of a specific anatomical region ( e.g. , lumbar vertebrae) can be embedded into the gelatin matrix. Indeed, the anechoic gelatin will allow the US beam to reach the surfaces of the plastic model that will appear as hyperechoic (similar to the cortical bone). 8 It would be convenient to position the plastic models considering the geometry of the needle insertion during practice ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab —Lumbar vertebra anatomical phantom. The plastic model is positioned in the container (A) and directly embedded in the gelatin (B). In this example, blue food coloring was used; therefore the solidified models (C) mimic human periosteum surrounded by hypo-/an-echoic substance. The US image (D) shows the lumbar vertebra in transverse plane. a: spinous process; b: facet joint; c: transverse process. Multiple convex-in-shape gelatin layers can be prepared to envelop the plastic model to reproduce body convexity and depth of the structures. In our case, a three-step layering process is employed, utilizing convex-shaped containers. Initial layer of the gelatin solution is pou\red into the container to establish a simulated (sub)cutaneous layer; therefore a higher concentration of psyllium fibers is suggested. This layer needs to be solidified via refrigeration (3-4 h). Yet, it will be the side that ultimately faces the superficial layer, mimicking the body surface, as the model will be utilized overturned. Upon achieving the desired firmness, additional gelatin is then carefully poured to embed the model. This new portion of the gelatin needs to be cooled down to 30-40 °C (86-104 °F) i.e. in order to be fluid enough to fill the voids, but in the meantime not melting the base layer. Upon completion of the final refrigeration step, the phantom has to be taken out of the container, inverted and used for training (Supplementary Digital Material 6: Supplementary Video 1, 1 min 54 s, 119 MB). Animal soft tissues, such as tendons and muscles, can be attached to the surfaces of the plastic models using cyanoacrylate (CA) glue – to reproduce the simulating human body architecture as accurately/much as possible. This way, one can obtain what is referred as bio-anatomical phantoms. Chicken breast and pork connective tissue/tendon can be used to simulate muscles and tendons or fibrocartilaginous structures, respectively. For sure, kitchen knives and surgical scalpels would be necessary while handling those tissues. An impregnated gauze or a higher concentrated psyllium layer can also be added superficially – to provide the sonographic pattern of a dermo-epidermal complex ( Figure 3 ). Eventually, more realistic phantoms mimicking the multi-layered architecture of human soft tissues i.e. , skin, subcutaneous tissue, and muscle can be reproduced. 11 , 15 , 16 Figure 3. Open in a new tab —Posterior shoulder joint bio-anatomical phantom. A) Production of the glenohumeral capsule and labrum with a dissected pork paratenon membrane (*). B) Production of the muscular layers with chicken breast. C) Solidified model. D) US image of the advanced shoulder phantom - mimicking the humeral head (1), glenoid (2), posterior labrum (3), infraspinatus (4) and deltoid (5) muscles, subcutaneous (6) and cutaneous (7) layers. Application After practicing the skills/techniques described in the basic part, these phantoms can be used for more complicated interventions. The wide spectrum includes – but is not confined to – lumbar spine facet injections ( Figure 4 ) (Supplementary Digital Material 7: Supplementary Video 2, 24 s, 8.88 MB), glenohumeral joint distension ( Figure 3 ) (Supplementary Digital Material 8: Supplementary Video 3, 18 s, 9.53 MB) (Supplementary Digital Material 9: Supplementary Video 4, 26 s, 12.4 MB) or Achilles tendon-paratenon hydro-dissection ( Figure 5 ) (Supplementary Digital Material 10: Supplementary Video 5, 36 s, 25.1 MB). Figure 4. Open in a new tab —Lumbar spine bio-anatomical phantom. A) Plastic lower spine model. B) Chicken breast attached to the plastic model mimicking the erector spinae muscle. C) The anatomical preparation embedded in gelatin. D) US imaging of the model shows the spinous process (1), facet joint (2), transverse process (3), erector spinae muscle (4), subcutaneous layer (5). E) An in-plane technique and lateral-to-medial approach can be used to inject the facet joint of the lumbar bio-anatomical phantom. Void arrowheads: needle. Figure 5. Open in a new tab —Production of the Achilles tendon using pork tendons. A) The pork tendons are already enveloped in a paratenon-like structure made of connective tissue. B) After the thin bottom layer of gelatin is solidified, three tendons are put in the same phantom to allow multiple simulations for hydro-dissection. C) US image of the Achilles tendon phantom whereby an interface is seen between the epitenon (void arrowheads) and paratenon (1). D) Training for hydro-dissection - the epitenon-paratenon interface is being filled with hypoechoic fluid by using an in-plane technique. 2: tendon tissue. Of note, the needle passage may sometimes generate a residual hyperechoic trace within the gel due to gas microbubbles entrapped inside. Filling the needle’s cavity with water, this drawback can be partially prevented during the training. To preserve the phantom’s structural integrity; it would be better to start with needle visualization exercises and to perform water bolus injections thereafter (preferably at the end). Discussion The use of US-G interventions has expanded dramatically, being an extremely useful tool in the physicians’ daily practice. Notably, their outcome is strictly dependent on skills – in addition to theoretical knowledge. Therefore, hands-on training before applying them in daily clinical practice is paramount. The use of cadavers remain as the most effective alternative in this sense, but they are often hindered by high cost and limited availability. 17 As such, US compatible phantoms have been used, ranging from simple gelatin-based models to sophisticated commercial simulators. 8 While traditional “home-made” gelatin models are cost-effective and simple to make, they often lack the complexity of human mechanical and anatomical characteristics. 18 Commercial phantoms, while offering higher fidelity, come with prohibitive costs and may not adequately represent the diversity of human tissues. Each option plays a role in education but also presents barriers to accessible and comprehensive training. Kwon et al. 19 demonstrated that using a gelatin-based spine phantom helped novices (with no experience of spinal US) acquire the skills necessary to perform US-G lumbar facet joint injections and medial branch blocks. 19 Likewise, Maloney et al. 20 reported better understanding of manual/technical skills ( e.g. rotation, sliding, and tilting of the US transducer) among 134 medical students – by using homemade, gelatin US puzzle phantoms. 20 Using scores based on the realism of US images and the needling process, Samuel et al. 21 defined pork, beef, and lamb joints to be effective models for simulation training. 21 Using homemade phantoms for training soft tissue biopsy and cyst aspiration, Giannotti et al. 22 studied a cohort of 22 residents and reported high satisfactory scores for both phantom production and use during the procedures. 22 Our methodological contribution to this evolving field encompasses a spectrum of phantom models, integrating the affordability and convenience of gelatin models with task specific structures. Basic principles of US-G procedures such as spatial exploration, needle visualization, injection, and aspiration can be practiced using the basic phantoms. Likewise, with the advanced phantoms, it is possible to provide target-specific simulations with anatomical and mechanical specificity close to that of cadaver practice. Moreover, our ‘recipe’ incorporates reusable materials to offer an ethical and cost-effective solution, thereby broadening the scope for extensive practice. Indeed, the plastic anatomical models can be reused by melting the gelatin, detaching fleshy parts, and putting them in a dishwasher. Interestingly, the gelatin itself can be reprocessed a few times, further enhancing the sustainability of these educational tools. Conclusions Overall, the need for training is paralleling the increase as regards the use of US-G procedures in musculoskeletal medicine. Herewith, the most common and realistic practice method i.e. cadavers are expensive and hardly accessible in different medical settings. As such, phantom-based training sessions appear to be excellent and easily available educational approach to boost the learning curve of musculoskeletal physicians. This article provides a comprehensive recipe or ‘cookbook’ for making various simple but effective phantoms – to be used at different steps of training for US-G interventions. In addition, we described instruments to customize phantoms for one or some specific tasks, according to the user’s need. The authors believe that such training and practice can have a significantly positive impact on the learning process of US-G procedures for junior or senior physicians alike. 23 , 24 Supplementary Digital Material 1 Supplementary Figure 1 Different types of basic phantoms. Supplementary_Digital_Material1.pdf (92.3KB, pdf) Supplementary Digital Material 2 Supplementary Figure 2 Spatial exploration using a basic phantom. Supplementary_Digital_Material2.pdf (208.6KB, pdf) Supplementary Figure 3 Spatial exploration using a basic phantom. Supplementary_Digital_Material2.pdf (208.6KB, pdf) Supplementary Digital Material 3 Supplementary Figure 4 Injection using a basic phantom. Supplementary_Digital_Material3.pdf (154.7KB, pdf) Supplementary Digital Material 4 Supplementary Figure 5 Aspiration using a basic phantom. Supplementary_Digital_Material4.pdf (170.6KB, pdf) Supplementary Digital Material 5 Supplementary Figure 6 Exemplary materials and objects for preparing the advanced phantoms. Supplementary_Digital_Material5.pdf (339.2KB, pdf) Supplementary Digital Material 6 Supplementary Video 1 Download video file (159.9MB, mp4) Supplementary Digital Material 7 Supplementary Video 2 Download video file (26MB, mp4) Supplementary Digital Material 8 Supplementary Video 3 Download video file (25MB, mp4) Supplementary Digital Material 9 Supplementary Video 4 Download video file (34.7MB, mp4) Supplementary Digital Material 10 Supplementary Video 5 Download video file (69.5MB, mp4) Footnotes Conflicts of interest: The authors certify that