INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7169,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8371081','Musculoskeletal Ultrasound as a Motivator for Selecting a Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Residency Program in the United States: A Multicenter Survey Study.','Raja AE, Shustorovich A, Robinson DM, Alfonso K, Meyer R, Roemmich RT, Eng C, Wisniewski SJ, Cabahug P.','2022','spaulding finnoff motivator spaulding rol motivator motivator demographically rol motivator motivator rol figure1 b239 rol motivator motivator rol b240 motivator b241 motivator rol rol matriculating amssm chemodenervation finoff proctored mksus sacrificing finnoff educationally spaulding demographically rol spaulding fulfills nihms1696548 supplementary_material_1 nihms1696548 supplementary_material_2 nihms1696548 supplementary_material_3 amelia proofreading altamash shustorovich spaulding kathryn roemmich spaulding cabahug cannella kissin torralba kaeley nazarian sayeed minardi kurdi werntz 340_physicalmedicinerehabilitation_2019 berko thornhill koenigsberg luz bockbrader acgmeweb faqpif 110_emergency_medicine_07012013 116_sportsmedicine_2019_tcc lanni asaad reinsel deveaux moten durkin e681 finnoff nutz grogg bahner royall bahner jasne boore cortez rempell saldana disalvo knobe munker sellei thiessen mew wessner potts auriemma whitehair motejunas bonneval finnoff kerkhof finnoff berkoff brakke boimbo sauerwein petriceks hales haring zaremski pillen alfen luz klabunde kellerman nihms1696548 supplementary_material_1 nihms1696548 supplementary_material_2 nihms1696548 supplementary_material_3','Abstract This study aimed to determine the influence of musculoskeletal ultrasound (MSKUS) curriculum on applicants during the residency-selection process. A survey of 666 applicants for the Johns Hopkins University, Mayo Clinic, and Harvard/Spaulding Rehabilitation Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation (PM&R) programs was conducted in June 2020. A total of 180 respondents scored the influence of a MSKUS curriculum on their decision-making for residency selection. Additionally, applicants were asked to rank specific areas of PM&R that influenced their decision making. Participants most commonly included MSKUS in their top three areas of interest when constructing their rank order list. When asked if MSKUS presence within a program had an effect during the interview-selection process, 71% responded with “very important” or “absolutely essential” (p<0.001). For 74% of applicants, exposure to MSKUS in residency was an important factor when creating their rank order list (p<0.001). Greater than 92% of applicants stated that they are “likely” or “very likely” to use MSKUS in their future practice and 83% would recommend a program with MSKUS to future candidates (p<0.001). Based on these results, a large percentage of PM&R applicants intend on using MSKUS in their future practice. Therefore, MSKUS may be an important factor for residency selection. Keywords: Musculoskeletal Ultrasound (MSKUS), Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (PM&R), Residency, Education, Recruitment INTRODUCTION Musculoskeletal ultrasound (MSKUS) is a versatile imaging modality for diagnosing musculoskeletal pathology in a variety of conditions and has become an extension of the clinician’s physical exam. The absence of radiation, relatively low cost, widespread availability, and dynamic capabilities of MSKUS have led to its increased use for diagnostic and interventional purposes over the last decade across multiple specialties, including orthopaedics, podiatry, rheumatology, sports medicine, and physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R). 1 , 2 Medicare data from 1996 to 2006 showed a 12,025% increase in ultrasound usage by musculoskeletal clinicians. 3 Sharpe et al. reported a further 316% increase in Medicare reimbursement from 2000–2009, with an associated 717% increase of outpatient office MSKUS guided procedures. 2 The authors also speculated that these numbers would continue to rise exponentially with current practice trends. 2 In 2015 the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) made it a requirement for PM&R training programs to expose residents to MSKUS via observing or performing 10 MSKUS procedures throughout their training. 4 Recent survey studies have shown formal MSKUS curricula to be present in at least 45% of PM&R residency programs, when accounting for survey nonresponders. 5 – 7 However, only 20% required demonstration of mastery for graduation. 6 , 7 Emergency medicine and sports medicine programs have established ultrasound as a core competency and require a formal curriculum for completion of residency and fellowship milestones by ACGME standards. 8 , 9 While not mandated by ACGME requirements, other subspecialties such as rheumatology and pain medicine have also started implementing MSKUS training into their educational curriculums. 10 , 11 In 2008, Finnoff et al. developed a comprehensive MSKUS curriculum that was successfully implemented at the Mayo Clinic PM&R residency program. 12 The program’s residents expressed a strong interest in becoming proficient with MSKUS and a comprehensive curriculum was regarded as a potential motivating factor to attract future applicants. 12 Current educational techniques include, but are not limited to, MSKUS didactics, hands-on workshops, and point-of-care MSKUS at bedside or teaching within outpatient clinics. 6 , 7 The use of ultrasound is growing in all specialties, with exposure starting as early as medical school. 13 – 17 A recent systematic review demonstrated a majority of medical students enjoy learning ultrasound, feel it should be part of their educational curricula, and have interest in pursuing ultrasound training in the future. 18 It is suspected that applicants value MSKUS exposure while evaluating PM&R residency programs, with one study showing that opportunities for hands-on procedural training to be the second most influential factor for applicants evaluating programs. 19 However, no formal studies have specifically evaluated the influence of MSKUS on program selection. The aim of this survey study was to determine how powerful of a motivator MSKUS is in decision making for residency selection. The authors predicted that MSKUS exposure/curriculum is highly influential for candidates when applying for PM&R residency. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study Design and Recruitment This multi-center study surveyed candidates who applied to PM&R residencies for the 2020 National Resident Matching Program (NRMP) at three different institutions: Johns Hopkins University, Mayo Clinic, and Spaulding Rehabilitation Hospital/Harvard Medical School. The primary aim was to evaluate the relative importance MSKUS exposure has on applicants’ residency selection process. Each participating Institutional Review Board deemed this study exempt. E-mail addresses of applicants were obtained by each institution from the program director workstation on Electronic Residency Application System (ERAS), with the permission of the Association of American Medical Colleges. In accordance with NRMP, and to minimize bias from fear that responses may influence an applicant’s rank position, the survey was sent in June, well after the 2020 Match was complete ( Figure 1 ). A total of 666 applicants were contacted to complete the survey, and a formal consent was obtained prior to survey initiation. To ensure confidentiality, survey responses were anonymous and no personal identifying information was shared between institutions. To avoid duplicate responses, applicants who applied to more than one of the study institutions were sent a single survey. Two follow-up emails were sent at one-week intervals and the final email reminder was sent one month after the initial email. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Survey questions. Demographic data questions not included. Outcome Measures The survey was initially designed as a quality improvement initiative by the primary author’s PM&R department. All authors reviewed and revised the survey with attention to response process until a final version was uniformly agreed upon. The first section consisted of four questions that aimed to quantify the importance of expected MSKUS exposure on interview selection, rank order list (ROL), future use in practice, and likelihood to recommend a program to others using a five-point Likert scale. These four questions were designated as “motivator” questions due to their direct influence on residency selection. Response options ranged from “0=of no importance” to “4=Absolutely essential” or “0=Very unlikely” to “4=Very likely.” A higher numerical response (≥3) was characterized as a positive response. The primary outcome variable was quantifying MSKUS as a motivator based on the Likert scale responses. Study participants were also asked about the importance of exposure to specific PM&R fields (traumatic brain injury, spinal cord injury, sports, pain, pediatrics, etc.) on residency selection. In the second section, applicants were asked to rank which type of structured MSKUS education they perceived as most important in a residency program: Dedicated MSKUS curriculum led by faculty, faculty who routinely use MSKUS in clinic, MSKUS lectures, resident-driven MSKUS curriculum, and opportunities to teach MSKUS to medical students. Several studies have demonstrated the value of these specific educational practices being utilized for ultrasound teaching. 6 , 7 , 16 , 17 As opposed to Likert values, ranking was selected to gauge the importance of one teaching method over another and provide actionable data to enhance residency curricula via a quality improvement initiative. Finally, applicants were asked optional demographic questions. Data Analysis Data analysis was performed using SPSS Statistics 26 and MATLAB on institutional computers. For the primary outcome, a one-sample chi-squared test of proportions was performed to compare the responses for each Likert value against chance. To compare whether the percent of responses for each Likert value were statistically different between the categories, Marginal Homogeneity Tests were performed. Fisher’s Exact Tests were performed to evaluate the effect of race and sex on participants. We also performed a series of secondary analyses. First, a two-sample chi-squared test of proportions was used to investigate whether participants who reported top areas of interest in interventional areas differed in their Likert values when compared to those who reported interest in non-interventional categories. Participants were assigned to the interventional subgroup if they reported top areas of interest in pain, interventional spine, and sports (n=10) or to the non-interventional subgroup if they did not report any of these disciplines as a top area of interest (n=18). For this secondary analysis, participants who reported MSKUS as a top area of interest (n=115) were omitted given that it was intuitive that they would report high Likert values on topics involving MSKUS exposure. Next, we used two-sample chi-squared tests of proportions to compare the demographics of our participants to the total cohort of ERAS applicants for 2020 in terms of sex and race/ethnicity to assess whether our sample was demographically representative of the population. Statistical significance for all analyses was defined a priori at p-value less than 0.05. RESULTS The survey was completed by 180 individuals, yielding a response rate of 27%. The average age of participants was 28.35 years. Of the respondents, 56 were female, 115 were male, and one responded as “other.” With respect to race, 92 respondents identified as White or Caucasian, 13 as Black or African American, 16 as Hispanic or Latino, 46 as Asian and 4 as “other.” MSKUS was considered “very important” or “absolutely essential” by 128 (71%) participants when evaluating programs for interview consideration ( Figure 2 ). Similarly, when participants were constructing their ROL, 134 (74%) respondents felt it was “very important” or “absolutely essential” to have a MSKUS curriculum ( Figure 2 ). When participants were asked how likely they were to use ultrasound in future practice, 167 (93%) responded “likely” or “very likely.” In response to the final motivator question, 150 (83%) participants reported that they would be “likely” or “very likely” to recommend a program with MSKUS to future candidates ( Figure 2 ). All chi-squared tests of proportions revealed that these percentages were significantly greater than those expected from random selection (all p<0.001). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Likert scale responses for the four motivator questions. Participants were asked how important a MSKUS curriculum was for determining where to interview (A), how important a MSKUS curriculum was when constructing a rank order list (B), how important would MSKUS be for their future practice (C), and how likely it was that they would recommend a PM&R program based on the presence of a MSKUS curriculum (n = 180; p<0.001 for all four questions). No significant differences were observed between the interventional and non-interventional subgroups in the proportions of Likert values for the interview (p=0.111), ROL (p=0.312), or recommendation (p=0.219) questions. The interventional group reported higher Likert values regarding the future practice question (p=0.024). With regards to how participants felt resident exposure to MSKUS should be implemented in residency programs, participants ranked the following from most to least important: dedicated ultrasound curriculum led by faculty, faculty who routinely use ultrasound in clinic, MSKUS lectures, resident driven ultrasound curriculum, and then opportunity to teach medical students ( Table 1 and Supplemental Content Figure1 , Supplemental Digital Content 1 , http://links.lww.com/PHM/B239 ). Table 1. Percentage of applicants who ranked the following aspects of education in order of importance. 1 = most important, 5 = least important. 1 2 3 4 5 Dedicated MSKUS curriculum led by faculty 63.3% 22.8% 6.7% 4.4% 0% Faculty who routinely use MSKUS in clinic 26.1% 46.1% 19.4% 4.4% 1.1% MSKUS Lectures 2.2% 15% 38.3% 27.8% 13.9% Resident-driven US curriculum 5% 10.6% 26.7% 41.1% 13.9% Opportunity to teach MSKUS to medical students 0.56% 2.8% 6.1% 19.4% 68.3% Open in a new tab Exposure to MSKUS was most often selected as one of the top three most important areas of PM&R residency education when determining the ROL by 115/180 respondents, followed by sports medicine (101/180) and pain medicine (66/180) ( Figure 3 ). The impact of these choices on the questions addressing MSKUS as a motivator was further analyzed with the Fisher’s Exact Tests. Survey participants who chose sports medicine as one of their three most important areas of interest were also more likely to respond positively to the MSKUS motivator questions regarding future use, interview, and ROL categories, but not to the recommendations category ( Supplemental Content Table 1 , Supplemental Digital Content 2 , http://links.lww.com/PHM/B240 ). Interest in pain medicine was also associated with a positive response to the MSKUS motivator questions but failed to show statistical significance ( Supplemental Content Table 2 , Supplemental Digital Content 3 , http://links.lww.com/PHM/B241 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Top participant interests. Participants were asked to choose their top three areas of interest within PM&R while creating their rank order list. There was a significant difference between sex, favoring the males to respond positively for all four motivator questions (p<0.05). Males were also less likely to demonstrate interest in pediatrics, but more likely to place interventional spine in their top three areas of interest (p=0.003; p=0.012). When comparing our sample to the 2020 ERAS applicant pool in terms of sex and race/ethnicity, we did not observe significant differences in biological sex (p=0.510) or in the proportions of participants identifying as Black or African American (p=0.264), Asian (p=0.368), or Hispanic, Latino, or of Spanish origin (p=0.997). 20 We did observe that the proportion of participants identifying as White was significantly larger in our sample when compared to the entire applicant pool (p=0.020). 20 DISCUSSION The purpose of this study was to assess the influence of expected MSKUS exposure during residency training on applicants’ program selection preferences. Based on the results of this study, exposure to MSKUS is a strong motivational factor when choosing a residency program and having a dedicated MSKUS curriculum as well as exposure to faculty who routinely use MSKUS appear to be the most preferred teaching methods for residency training. In fact, 64% of participants listed MSKUS as one of the most important factors in choosing a residency program, followed by sports medicine (56%) and then pain medicine (37%). Notably, applicants interested in sports or pain medicine are likely to use MSKUS in their future clinical practices. 21 – 24 When evaluating programs to interview for residency placement, 71% of candidates considered MSKUS “very important” or “absolutely essential.” This number increased to 74% while constructing their ROL, reinforcing MSKUS as a necessary component of training. Furthermore, if considering those who ranked MSKUS of “average importance” while creating their ROL, that number increases to a remarkable 93%. Perhaps the most interesting finding of this study was that most applicants (93% of respondents) intend on using MSKUS in their future practice. This highlights the value of gaining competency during residency and parallels the interests and career patterns of currently practicing physiatrists. Contemporary survey studies have revealed that as many as 80% of graduating residents matriculating into fellowship, matched pain, interventional spine, or sports medicine, with trends over the past two decades supporting sports medicine as one of the most sought-after post-graduate specialties. 25 – 27 The American Medical Society for Sports Medicine (AMSSM) requires mandatory training and development of a strong curriculum in MSKUS during fellowship. 23 Recent literature has even supported that early experience with MSKUS may play a role for acceptance into a sports medicine fellowship program. 28 Thus, PM&R applicants that ultimately plan to pursue sports medicine will continue to seek out opportunities to acquire early training in MSKUS. 24 , 28 Although pain, sports medicine, and interventional spine fellowships continue to be popular career paths of graduating PM&R residents, the utility of MSKUS extends beyond these subspecialties. 27 MSKUS also plays a critical role in diagnosing neuromuscular disorders, electrodiagnostic studies, guidance for chemodenervation procedures, and in pediatrics due to its lack of ionizing radiation. 29 , 30 Additionally, many PM&R graduates go into practice without completing a fellowship and thus their ability to utilize MSKUS in clinic is highly dependent on the education they receive in residency. 27 The utility of MSKUS spanning the breadth of the specialty may explain the disproportionately overwhelming response of applicants in this study who intend on using MSKUS in their future practice. Finoff et al. revealed that 100% of the residents queried responded positively to the survey question of whether they would recommend a residency program with a MSKUS course. 12 Interestingly, the majority of applicants in this study also stated they would recommend a PM&R program with MSKUS to future applicants, which suggests an inherent interest in MSKUS despite lack of hands-on time and clinical experience. Nevertheless, incorporation of ultrasound education within medical school curricula has exposed students to its widespread utility and increased the demand for pursuing further ultrasound training in their future. 15 , 18 The results of this survey study suggest that a formalized MSKUS curriculum, ideally structured with assessments to demonstrate competence, may enhance recruitment for PM&R residency programs. Respondents reported that a dedicated ultrasound curriculum led by faculty was the most preferred MSKUS educational method. A faculty-driven curriculum would involve educational sessions guided and proctored by faculty who routinely use MKSUS in their practice. We suspect respondents preferred faculty-driven over resident-driven curricula due to faculty’s greater experience using MSKUS clinically, their ability to provide troubleshooting, and their sessions being perceived as higher yield with less self-directed learning. Incorporating teaching in clinics by faculty who use ultrasound was also highly valued, with 161 (89%) of the respondents ranking some form of faculty teaching as the most important factor regarding MSKUS training in residency, further highlighting the importance applicants place on learning from those they perceive as “experts.” Notably, there are many barriers to creating a formal MSKUS curriculum, which include, but are not limited to; adequate faculty expertise, lack of equipment, inadequate literature defining educational curricula, and sacrificing training in other areas of PM&R in an already comprehensive training program. 7 Finnoff et al. created a model at the Mayo clinic in 2008 for formal MSKUS training that can be utilized as a resource when designing or implementing a MSKUS curriculum. 12 Other studies have also provided practical and feasible methods for designing a MSKUS curriculum such as team based learning, peer teaching led by senior residents, and the use of MSKUS milestones. 7 , 31 , 32 Based on the results of our survey, programs looking to enhance their current MSKUS education or implement a new curriculum would benefit from recruiting faculty with proficient MSKUS experience and/or experience teaching. Alternatively, MSKUS training for faculty may be implemented to increase MSKUS utilization and trainee exposure during regular clinical duties. Formal MSKUS lectures was the third most important educational modality according to our survey study. It is unclear whether didactics are routinely given by PM&R program faculty, residents or in combination, but both faculty-led and peer-directed MSKUS training have been found to be educationally beneficial. 16 , 17 Peer teaching also allows the implementation of MSKUS within residency curricula more easily and has been shown to reinforce a level of competency, which may allow residents to feel more prepared to perform these diagnostic and interventional procedures after graduation. 6 , 31 , 32 Therefore, this may be a viable method for MSKUS training when faculty availability is limited. The main limitation of this study is the relatively low response rate, despite consideration of several factors affecting response rates when formulating the survey, such as survey design, clear articulation of questions and response options, and survey length (shorter surveys have higher response rates). 33 , 34 Follow-up reminders also improve survey response rates, but appear to be less effective for web-based surveys compared to mail surveys. 35 Another notable limitation is the potential response bias which is inherent to surveys. 34 This was an optional survey sent to all PM&R applicants for the Johns Hopkins, Mayo Clinic, and Harvard/Spaulding PM&R programs, and thus a selection bias may also exist. Similarly, two of the programs only offered PGY-2 positions, which potentially compounded selection bias. Given that the motivations of applicants who chose to respond to the survey may contrast from those who did not respond, the response rate represents a non-randomized bias and limits the generalizability of the results. However, this limitation was addressed with demographic analysis of our sample population. Participants in this study were demographically similar to the ERAS applicant pool with respect to both sex and race/ethnicity, with the exception that our sample had a slightly larger proportion of participants identifying as White. Thus, this sample is likely representative of the general pool of applicants for the 2020 match cycle. Because this survey was administered at the end of the applicants’ fourth year of medical school, a possibility for recall bias cannot be ignored. Additionally, applicants were not asked to rank their answers for the top three most important areas of PM&R when constructing their ROL. This limited the analysis as applicants may have viewed all three of their selections as equally important. Regarding the question assessing which types of MSKUS education were preferable; possible additional answers such as funding to attend outside courses were not included, and thus we may not have captured other important ways of learning MSKUS. Finally, an option to rank preferred educational practices equally was not available. Although the eligible applicants for this study were selected from only three PM&R residency programs, there is a high likelihood that the majority of the PM&R candidates applying for the PGY-2 positions were contacted. During the 2020 NRMP match cycle, 628 candidates applied for PGY-2 positions, 550 applied for PGY-1 positions, and 33 applied for Reserved positions. 35 Of the institutions participating in this study, the Johns Hopkins University and Harvard/Spaulding PM&R programs only offered PGY-2 positions during the 2020 cycle whereas the Mayo Clinic offered both PGY-1 (categorical) and PGY-2 (advanced) positions. Out of the 666 applicants contacted, 486 did not apply to the Mayo Clinic. When considering the remaining 180 applicants, it may be safe to assume that many of them applied for both PGY-1 and PGY-2 positions. Therefore, it is plausible that the majority, if not all, of the 628 candidates applying for and ranking PGY-2 positions were included in this study. While 1081 applicants applied to PM&R residency programs according to ERAS, we elected to use NRMP data as it reflected the total number of applicants who certified a rank list for a PM&R position in the 2020 match. Thus, the NRMP data likely represents a more accurate measure of prospective physiatrists and fulfills the purpose of this study. 20 , 37 It is worth noting that the NRMP does not collect demographic data, and thus we used ERAS data to highlight the demographic similarities of this study with the entire applicant pool. 38 Despite the promising results of this survey study, further research is necessary to identify potential motivational factors for PM&R residency selection, particularly the value of MSKUS. Developing a post-match survey administered by the ACGME, NRMP or a PM&R governing body would further validate the results of this study. CONCLUSION The vast majority of PM&R applicants in this study intend on using MSKUS in their future practice and seek to achieve competency in training. For this reason, MSKUS exposure/curriculum was an important factor for residency selection among these applicants. Faculty involvement in MSKUS training appears to be the most sought-after educational practice and can enhance residency curricula. Most PM&R educators agree that MSKUS is well within the scope of physiatry training and should be implemented within residency curricula. These findings suggest that MSKUS interest among physiatrists may continue to grow and become ubiquitous. Therefore, for the residency programs who do not already have these resources in place, it would be important to create a formalized MSKUS curriculum and/or strengthen their current curriculum to improve the residency recruitment process. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1696548-supplement-Supplementary_Material_1.docx (49.3KB, docx) Supplementary Material 2 NIHMS1696548-supplement-Supplementary_Material_2.docx (15.6KB, docx) Supplementary Material 3 NIHMS1696548-supplement-Supplementary_Material_3.docx (15.1KB, docx) Acknowledgements: We thank Amelia S. Ahmed, DO, MS for her expertise and assistance with technical editing, language editing, and proofreading. Footnotes Author Disclosures: None of the authors of this manuscript have any competing interests. No funding was obtained for this project from any source and none of the authors stand to gain any financial benefit from this manuscript. An abstract of this manuscript has been submitted for the Association of Academic Physiatrists Annual Meeting. Contributor Information Altamash E. Raja, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, The Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine.. Alexander Shustorovich, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, The Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine.. David Robinson, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Spaulding Rehabilitation Hospital/Harvard Medical School.. Kathryn Alfonso, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Mayo Clinic School of Graduate Medical Education.. Ryan Meyer, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, The Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine.. Ryan T. Roemmich, Center for Movement Studies, Kennedy Krieger Institute. Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, The Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine.. Christine Eng, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Spaulding Rehabilitation Hospital/Harvard Medical School.. Steve J. Wisniewski, Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, Mayo Clinic School of Graduate Medical Education.. Philippines Cabahug, International Center for Spinal Cord Injury, Kennedy Krieger Institute. Department of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation, The Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine.. References 1. Cannella AC, Kissin EY, Torralba KD, Higgs JB, Kaeley GS. Evolution of musculoskeletal ultrasound in the United States: Implementation and practice in rheumatology. Arthritis Care Res 2014;66(1):7–13. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sharpe RE, N