ndman A, Ling PM, Glantz SA. Tobacco industry youth smoking prevention programs: protecting the industry and hurting tobacco control. Am J Public Health 2002;92:917–30. 10.2105/AJPH.92.6.917 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Lauer J, Sassi F, Vigo A et al. Health Taxes: Policy and Practice. World Scientific, 2023. [ Google Scholar ] Ritchie J, Spencer L. Qualitative data analysis for applied policy research. In: Huberman A, Miles M (eds.), The Qualitative Researcher''s Companion. Thousand Oaks, California: SAGE Publications Inc, 2002. [ Google Scholar ] Rukanga B. What are Kenya''s controversial tax proposals? BBC News, 2024. 25 June 2024. [ Google Scholar ] Singh A, Smith K, Hellowell M et al. An exploration of stakeholder views and perceptions on taxing tobacco, alcohol and sugar-sweetened beverages in Ghana. BMJ Glob Health 2023;8:e012054. 10.1136/bmjgh-2023-012054 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Smith J, Firth J. Qualitative data analysis: the framework approach. Nurse Res 2011;18:52–62. 10.7748/nr2011.01.18.2.52.c8284 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] VALD . Ghana: VALD Steps Up Advocacy Campaign for Alcohol, Tobacco Taxation , 2020. https://valdgh.org/ghana-vald-steps-up-advocacy-campaign-for-alcohol-tobacco-taxation/ (10 August 2024, date last accessed). Vision for Alternative Development. Weiss JA. The powers of problem definition: the case of government paperwork. Policy Sciences 1989;22:97–121. 10.1007/BF00141381 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] WHO . Non-communicable Diseases Country Profile 2018. Geneva , 2018. https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/country-profiles/ncds/gha_en.pdf?sfvrsn=c60fe16b_45&download=true (10 August 2024, date last accessed). World Health Organization. World Bank . Taxes on Sugar-Sweetened Beverages: International Evidence and Experiences. Health, Nutrition and Population. Washington DC and online , 2020. https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/d9612c480991c5408edca33d54e2028a-0390062021/original/World-Bank-2020-SSB-Taxes-Evidence-and-Experiences.pdf (10 August 2024, date last accessed). World Bank Group. World Health Organization . Ghana: Empowering Health Workers and Mobilizing Communities to Improve Early Detection and Treatment of Noncommunicable Diseases in Ghana , 2022. https://www.who.int/about/accountability/results/who-results-report-2020-mtr/country-story/2022/ghana–empowering-health-workers-and-mobilizing-communities-to-improve-early-detection-and-treatment-of-noncommunicable-diseases-in-ghana#:∼:text=In%20Ghana%2C%2045%25%20of%20all,in%20collaboration%20with%20WHO%20offices (10 August 2024, date last accessed). WHO. Wright A, Smith KE, Hellowell M. Policy lessons from health taxes: a systematic review of empirical studies. BMC Public Health 2017;17:583. 10.1186/s12889-017-4497-z [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Young B, Lewis S, Katikireddi SV et al. Effectiveness of mass media campaigns to reduce alcohol consumption and harm: a systematic review. Alcohol Alcohol 2018;53:302–16. 10.1093/alcalc/agx094 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials czaf042_Supplementary_Data czaf042_supplementary_data.docx (14.9MB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data underlying this article cannot be shared publicly due to the terms of the consent agreement, which offered participants anonymity as the full transcripts include details that could identify some individuals. Further aspects of the data will be shared on reasonable request to the Principle Investigator and co-author, Arti Singh ( artisingh_uk@yahoo.com ).',31,'2025-10-20 06:53:37','2025-10-20 06:53:37');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7188,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11488140','A call for improving of musculoskeletal education on physical medicine and rehabilitation studies: a systematic review with meta-analysis.','Jin Y, Ma L, Zhou J, Xiong B, Fernando A, Snelgrove H.','2024','crd42024563899 afflict crd42024563899 noexp noexp noexp subheading houseofficer arbitrated delved haenszel dersimonianlaird dimensioning barbados prosections vioreanu hecimovich truntzer sayeed postmodule perrig pino dislocated prosection mitrousias peeler prosection anantharaman carpentier loupes loupes miedany karhade larrivee barbados zabel zabel zabel mutua apco mitrousias prosected narnaware crenshaw npt lydon safmeds oberoi rudolphi solero apco npt safmeds shuffled symmetrically excludeding majoring prosection prosections vivid delving lqm harkins 185x acb rayman s12891 lydon gannon safmeds asahi briganti jaoa s2665 berh greising woodley curci pisano bonacci postcoronavirus balta soames embalmed estai bunt aanat waterbrook suffoletto jsr tabassum bharatha vioreanu shelly s11845 haberstroh antolic hecimovich volet truntzer barua sayeed marrocco sully pizzimenti javoroski matzkin gartshore nepr perrig berendonk rogausch sostmann schrieber eyles 185x pino relocations feeley mitrousias varitimidis hantes prosected aanat peeler bulow aanat anantharaman ravindranath dayal osteology s00276 sostmann otsr carpentier aubeux armengol loupes jde miedany gaafary aroussy karhade qudsi usoro larrivee rodger larouche vanhomwegen dow arrct zabel sterz hoefer fammed mitrousias karachalios varitimidis prosected narnaware neumeier zargaran kokotkin s252274 crenshaw piazza stubley hashmi cots oberoi reynaga hinojosa rudolphi hagiwara iwakura sucharew gamified kahoot wijenayake s40670 s40670 digiovanni southgate e185 mcdaniel forlenza saif fida mansoor pjms malliaras merolli msksp cottrell msksp budhathoki','Abstract Background The incidence of musculoskeletal diseases is increasing in clinical practice, leading to a growing demand for rehabilitation medicine. However, education in musculoskeletal education is lacking in the training of physical medicine and rehabilitation students. This study aims to evaluate the impact of musculoskeletal education on medical students’ learning of rehabilitation medicine and analyze the correlation between influencing factors and education. Objectives The primary objective of this study is to evaluate the impact of musculoskeletal education on medical students’ learning of rehabilitation medicine. Furthermore, the authors aim to analyze the correlation between various influencing factors and the effectiveness of this education, with the goal of informing future educational interventions and enhancing student outcomes. Methods A comprehensive search was conducted across the Pubmed, Ovid, and Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (CENTRAL) databases for relevant articles published from January 2012 to September 2024. The search focused on medical students as the research subjects and musculoskeletal education as the intervention method. Both randomized controlled trials (RCTs) and non-randomized controlled trials (non-RCTs) were included. The selected studies underwent a systematic review, and meta-analysis was performed using R 3.4.4 software. Results After a thorough search, 41 studies were included. The retrospective study indicated that the most common impacts were knowledge acquisition, self-confidence, and satisfaction. The subsequent meta-analysis revealed significant increases in students’ knowledge acquisition [standardized mean differences (SMD) = 2.15, 95% confidence interval [CI] (1.36, 2.94), I 2 = 97%, random effect model] and self-confidence [SMD = 5.81, 95% CI (2.88, 8.75), I 2 = 97%, random effect model]. Compared with the general teaching control group, the knowledge acquisition of students in the observation group improved [SMD = 0.25, 95% CI (0.00, 0.51), I 2 = 83%, in the random effect model]; However, no significant difference in satisfaction occurred [SMD = 0.27, 95% CI (-0.47, 1.01), P > 0.05, I 2 = 89%, in the random effects model]. Conclusion Musculoskeletal education primarily impacts knowledge acquisition self-confidence, and satisfaction among physical medicine and rehabilitation students. The meta-analysis further establishes that musculoskeletal education significantly enhances knowledge acquisition and confidence in these students. Trial registration CRD42024563899 (completed on July 17, 2024). Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-024-06178-4. Keywords: Musculoskeletal education, Physical medicine and rehabilitation, Medical students, Retrospective study, Meta-analysis Introduction Musculoskeletal education is a branch of anatomy that encompasses the study of rheumatism and skeletal muscle health and disease [ 1 ]. Conditions such as gout, low back pain, osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and osteoporosis are prevalent in clinical practice [ 2 ]. In the United States, musculoskeletal disorders affect 33.3% of the adult population, compared to the combined rates of cardiovascular diseases and chronic respiratory conditions [ 3 ]. The prevalence extends globally and is notably associated with concurrent chronic diseases including cardiovascular disease, cancer, diabetes, chronic respiratory disease, and obesity [ 4 ]. Epidemiological studies reveal that musculoskeletal disorders afflict 20–33% of the global population, impacting more than 163 million individuals worldwide [ 5 ]. Subsequently, this poses a significant global burden regarding health, social, and economic costs. Owing to population aging, the prevalence of musculoskeletal disorders is projected to increase. Research demonstrates the benefits of rehabilitation for patients with such disorders [ 6 ], including alleviating pain, addressing structural abnormalities, and mitigating dysfunction from acute or chronic injuries or disease. This, in turn, improves patients’ overall quality of life and facilitates their integration into family and society [ 7 ]. Therefore, musculoskeletal education is integral to rehabilitation science [ 8 ]. Notably, over half of patients with severe COVID-19 required rehabilitation after discharge [ 9 ]. The impact of the epidemic has increased the incidence rate of musculoskeletal related diseases, especially in the elderly [ 10 ]. Therefore, there is an increasing necessity for qualified doctors to manage musculoskeletal diseases persists. All graduated doctors should be able to describe and recognize the pathophysiological state of the musculoskeletal system; specifically, they should have a proficient level of musculoskeletal knowledge [ 3 ]. Because students have very limited exposure to anatomy during their clinical training, medical students are poorly prepared for anatomy at the time of their residency program [ 11 , 12 ]. In the emergency department, for example, musculoskeletal and sports medicine are underrepresented in U.S. medical school curricula, with only 15% of schools requiring clinical musculoskeletal internships. This results in medical students entering residency programs with a lack of knowledge in this area [ 13 ]. UK medical schools are currently failing to adequately train medical students to manage orthopaedic patients, with students reporting low ability scores for all essential trauma and orthopedics skills [ 14 ]. Furthermore, it has been observed that a significant gap persists between the education provided in musculoskeletal anatomy and the practical application of this knowledge in rehabilitation settings. This discrepancy is concerning as it can hinder the ability of healthcare professionals to deliver effective and comprehensive treatment plans for patients with musculoskeletal injuries or conditions. Research conducted by Singh et al. [ 15 ] highlights this issue, emphasizing the need for a more integrated approach to musculoskeletal education and rehabilitation teaching in order to bridge this gap and improve patient outcomes. The impact of this on the rehabilitation profession is undoubtedly negative. Therefore, it is crucial to explore the influence of musculoskeletal education on learning rehabilitation medicine and evaluate the correlation between its influencing factors and teaching. Methods This research conducted a systematic review with meta-analysis to synthesize the available evidence on the effectiveness of musculoskeletal education in physical medicine and rehabilitation studies. The systematic review process involved a comprehensive search of electronic databases, screening of identified studies for eligibility, and data extraction from included studies. The meta-analysis component aimed to quantitatively pool the results of eligible studies to assess the overall effect of musculoskeletal education on relevant outcomes, such as level of knowledge acquisition, gain of confidence, satisfaction with knowledge. This approach was chosen to provide a comprehensive and rigorous evaluation of the current state of research in this area and to identify gaps in knowledge that can inform future research and practice. This study was executed and its findings were presented adhering to the standards outlined in the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) Statement [ 16 ]. Furthermore, the study protocol was formally registered in PROSPERO under the unique identifier CRD42024563899. Data sources Pubmed, Ovid, and Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (CENTRAL) were systematically searched, and the period considered was from January 2012 to September 2024. Only articles published in peer-review journals and written in the English language were included. No restrictions for ethnicity, gender, age, and setting were observed. Search strategy The keywords used during the search are as follows: “Education, Medical“[Mesh: noexp] OR “Education, Medical, Graduate“[Mesh] OR “Education, Medical, Continuing“[Mesh: noexp] OR “Internship and Residency“[Mesh] OR “Specialty Boards“[Mesh] OR “Education“[Mesh: noexp] OR “education“[Subheading] OR resident[tiab] OR residents[tiab] OR residency[tiab] OR PGY*[tiab] OR “post graduate year“[tiab] OR “postgraduate year“[tiab] OR trainee*[tiab] OR intern[tiab] OR interns[tiab] OR internship*[tiab] OR “house officer“[tiab] OR “house officers“[tiab] OR houseofficer*[tiab] OR “house staff“[tiab] OR housestaff[tiab] OR “medical education“[tiab] OR “training program“[tiab] OR “training programs“[tiab] OR “education program“[tiab] OR “education programs“[tiab] OR professionalism[tiab] OR “board certification“[tiab] OR “specialty boards“[tiab]) AND (Musculoskeletal[tiab]. Additionally, a manual search of references in related literature was conducted (Supplemental File Table 1 ). Primary outcomes Level of knowledge acquisition: an objective or subjective measure of the knowledge gained by participants related to musculoskeletal health, including diagnosis, treatment, and rehabilitation protocols. Secondary outcomes Gain of confidence: an assessment of the increase in confidence participants experience in managing musculoskeletal conditions or executing rehabilitative techniques post-intervention. Satisfaction with knowledge: a subjective measure of the participant’s overall satisfaction with the educational content and its relevance to their musculoskeletal healthcare needs or professional development. Given the substantial variation in the measurement ranges employed by the various studies (ranging from a five-point to a hundred-mark system), direct aggregation of the continuous variables would have compromised the accuracy and validity of the overall assessment of teaching outcomes. To address this issue, the authors employed SMD as a method to de-dimension the data, allowing for a more precise and unbiased evaluation of the combined results. By standardizing the variables, the authors mitigated the impact of differing scales and ensured that the merged data reflected a true representation of the underlying phenomena, thus strengthening the rigor and scientific validity of the analysis. Selection criteria The authors included studies if they met the following criteria: Population: participants undergoing medical training, encompassing medical students (including undergraduate students and interns) as well as residents. Intervention: there are studies where the observation group undergoes medical education utilizing innovative methods such as Problem-Based Learning (PBL) or similar approaches, while the control group receives conventional education. Alternatively, there are studies without a control group may be conducted, focusing on the comparison of outcomes before and after the implementation of medical education. Control: Receives conventional education, without the introduction of any experimental or innovative instructional techniques. Outcomes: six core competencies including professionalism, patient care, medical knowledge, interpersonal and communication skills, systems-based care, and practice-based learning and improvement. Study design: The research incorporates both RCTs and non-RCTs, allowing for a comprehensive evaluation of the intervention’s effectiveness across different study designs. The authors excluded studies that: (1) The intervention measures have nothing to do with relevant medical education. (2) Lack of clear outcome indicators. (3) Review studies. (4) Publications in languages other than English. Two authors systematically screened the literature according to the inclusion and exclusion criteria. Data extraction Data extraction was performed using an electronic data collection form created specifically for this review. Study characteristics were extracted by two independent authors. Kappa value was used to evaluate the consistency of data extraction between the two groups.In case of ambiguity and disagreement after research discussions, a third researcher arbitrated. When Kappa = 1, it indicates that the data presented by the two groups are completely consistent, and the data can be used for the final analysis. The content of literature extraction included author, year, place, grouping, teaching intervention models, and results. Thereafter, RCTs were retrieved by the JADAD and non-RCTs were retrieved by the Newcastle-Ottawa Scale (NOS) to account for selected criteria, such as selection, comparability, and outcome, to evaluate the quality of the experimental design [ 17 ]. Primary and secondary outcomes Despite the acknowledged importance of musculoskeletal education in medical training, the literature reveals that only a limited number of studies have delved into its influence on medical students’ learning outcomes. Specifically, these studies have explored the extent to which musculoskeletal education on medical students’ learning, such as the degree of knowledge mastery, skill acquisition, and learning attitude of students [ 1 ]. Given this scarcity of research, the present study aims to contribute to the field by focusing on a crucial aspect of medical students’ learning: the level of knowledge acquisition pertaining to musculoskeletal anatomy. The primary outcome o f this study is the level of knowledge acquisition among medical students in the domain of musculoskeletal anatomy. This outcome will be evaluated through assessing students’ comprehension and recognition of this anatomical area, encompassing both the depth of their knowledge mastery and their proficiency in applying this knowledge in practical situations. Typically, this assessment will involve examining students’ scores on relevant tests or evaluations, providing a quantitative measure of their learning outcomes in this critical area. The secondary outcomes: in addition to assessing the primary outcome of knowledge acquisition, this study will also examine two secondary outcomes that are equally crucial in evaluating the effectiveness of musculoskeletal education among medical students. Firstly, gain of confidence, among participants, which measures the extent to which they experience an increase in self-assurance and perceived competence in managing musculoskeletal conditions or executing rehabilitative techniques following the intervention. This outcome captures the change in their ability to apply their knowledge and skills confidently in real-world scenarios related to musculoskeletal healthcare. Secondly, satisfaction with knowledge, focusing on their overall satisfaction with the educational content provided and its relevance to their musculoskeletal healthcare needs or professional development. This outcome aims to capture their perception of the value and usefulness of the information they have learned, providing insights into how well the educational intervention met their expectations and needs. Methodological quality assessment For non-RCTs, the NOS was utilized as a standardized tool to assess the quality of the studies. For RCTs, the JADAD scale, a well-established and validated instrument, was adopted for evaluating the quality of the RCTs. Moreover, to strengthen the rigor of the RCT evaluation, the assessment was extended beyond the JADAD scale by incorporating the Cochrane Collaboration’s Risk of Bias (RoB) tool (Supplemental File Figs. 1 and 2 ). The creation of the RoB figure was facilitated using RevMan V5 (Cochrane). Bias test of publication A funnel plot was used to evaluate the bias for both RCTs and non-RCTs. When the funnel plot was asymmetric, the publication bias was assessed visually by funnel plots, and statistically by a regression asymmetry test (Egger’s test) (Supplemental File Table 2 ). Statistical analysis Heterogeneity across studies was assessed using the I 2 inconsistency test and chi-square-based Cochran’s Q statistic test in which P < 0.05 indicates the presence of significant heterogeneity. The fixed-effect model (Mantel-Haenszel method) was used to calculate the pooled SMD when substantial heterogeneity was not observed. In cases of potential heterogeneity, the random-effects model (DerSimonianLaird method) was adopted. As the indicators to be merged have different measurement ranges (such as the hundred-mark, ten-point, and five-point systems), the continuous variables were combined using the standardized method for de-dimensioning. The final result was reported with SMD with 95% CI. All P -values were two-sided and P < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Subgroup analysis was performed according to the study design (RCTs and non-RCTs). R 3.4.4 software was used to report outcomes. Sensitivity analysis One article was eliminated sequentially, and the remaining articles (n-1) were pooled for the meta-analysis. By observing the changes in the combined results, the original meta-analysis results were evaluated to determine whether the results were significantly changed by some studies. Result Study selection In total, 2052 articles were identified through a computer search, manual screening, and preliminary search. After removing articles with poor quality and high repetition, 84 articles were left for secondary screening. Of them, 8 articles with unclear comparison group and 35 articles without final outcomes were excluded. Of the remaining 41 [ 3 , 15 , 18 – 56 ] articles were selected and included in the study. Figure 1 PRISMA flowchart. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab PRISMA flowchart Study characteristics All included studies were of high-quality with scores according to the NOS (ranging from 6 to 9 ) and JADAD (ranging from 2 to 4 ) criteria. Table 1 summarizes the study characteristics, designs, and findings of the 41 included articles. Among them, 10 were published in 2019; 7 in 2021; 5 in 2020; 4 in 2018, 2015, and 2014; 3 in 2017; 2 in 2016; and 1 in 2022 and 2013. Eight studies were from the United States; 4 each from the UK, Ireland, Germany, and Canada; 3 from Australia; 2 each from Greece, Spain, and Sweden; and the remainder from Barbados, Belgium, China, Colombia, France, India, Japan, and Malaysia. Of the 41 articles, a total of 46 studies used different interventions. 21 studies had control and observation groups, of the included studies, 29 employed a before-and-after control design, which compared outcomes before and after an intervention in both a control group and a treatment group. 5 studies involved musculoskeletal-related courses [ 18 , 22 , 30 , 41 , 47 ], and 5 employed online simulations of human musculoskeletal [ 27 , 32 , 34 , 48 , 54 ] and practical operation of prosections [ 26 , 29 , 35 , 37 , 46 ]. Other studies used strategies such as flipped classroom [ 39 , 49 , 52 ], mutual evaluation of students [ 20 , 36 , 55 ], and podcasts [ 29 , 37 ]. Finally, musculoskeletal education predominantly affected physical medicine and rehabilitation students in three key areas: knowledge acquisition, self-confidence, and satisfaction.(funding sources see in Supplemental File Table 3 ). Table 1. Characteristics of included studies First author Year Country Intervention group Sample size (Intervention) Control group Sample size (Control) Main outcomes # NOS/JADAD score Vioreanu MH et al.[ 18 ] 2013 Ireland 2-week interactive musculoskeletal 140 / / 1 6 Back DA et al.[ 19 ] 2014 Germany PBL 35 non-users 18 1 7 Hecimovich M et al.[ 20 ] 2014 Australia Peer patient 22 standardised 21 1, 2 9 Kelly M et al.[ 21 ] 2014 Ireland One week with the experts 154 / / 1 7 Truntzer J et al.[ 22 ] 2014 USA Musculoskeletal educational practices 13 Control group 190 3 7 Mitra NK et al.[ 23 ] 2015 Malaysia Online formative MCQ tests with automated feedback 65 Paper-based formative 102 1 3* Sayeed Y et al.[ 24 ] 2015 USA OM 3 / / 1 7 Shields RK et al.[ 25 ] 2015 USA Near-peer teaching 56 Control group 33 3 7 Weiss K et al.[ 26 ] 2015 USA Postmodule group 296 Control group 109 1 8 Blake H et al.[ 27 ] 2016 UK Online learning tool 88 / / 1 7 