arnaware YR et al.[ 47 ] 2020 Canada MSK POCUS 10 / / 1 7 Wilson G et al.[ 48 ] 2020 UK VR 45 Physical model 44 1 3* Wu S et al.[ 49 ] 2020 China Flipped classroom 55 / / 1 7 Crenshaw ML et al.[ 50 ] 2021 USA Musculoskeletal boot camp course 58 / / 2 6 Kumar PR et al.[ 51 ] 2021 UK NPT 61 / / 1, 2 7 Lydon S et al.[ 3 ] 2021 Ireland SAFMEDS 14 Usual teaching 17 1 4* Oberoi MK et al.[ 52 ] 2021 USA Flipped classroom 64 / / 1 7 Ortega P et al.[ 53 ] 2021 Spanish An 8-hour educational module 47 / / 1, 2 6 Rudolphi-Solero T et al.[ 54 ] 2021 Spain Virtual world second life 32 / / 1 6 Yamauchi K et al.[ 55 ] 2021 Japan Peer role-playing 64 Control group 26 1 7 Schultz K et al.[ 56 ] 2022 USA Intervention group 46 Control group 27 1 3* Open in a new tab *JADAD score # main outcomes: 1 = knowledge, 2 = confidence, 3 = satisfaction Abbreviations USA: United States of America; UK: United Kingdom; PBL: problem-based learning; MCQ: multiple choice question; OM: occupational medicine; ILAR: international league of associations for rheumatology; PAL: peer-assisted learning; MSK POCUS: musculoskeletal point of care ultrasound; APCO: advanced primary care orthopedics; VR: virtual reality; NPT: near-peer teaching; SAFMEDS: say-all-fast-minute-everyday-shuffled Effects of interventions Level of knowledge acquisition Evaluated using pre- and post-tests, objective exams, or surveys that quantify the extent of knowledge gained on specific topics related to musculoskeletal health. Scores on these assessments will be used to determine the effectiveness of the educational intervention in transferring knowledge to participants. With a control group. A total of 19 articles reported knowledge differences between the observation and control groups, of which 6 studies had more than one observation group; thus, 27 comparison groups were used to analyze the knowledge difference between the observation and control groups. The results showed that SMD was 0.25 (95% CI = 0.00, 0.51) [ I 2 = 83%, in the random effects model], subgroup analysis revealed in RCTs [SMD = 0.49, 95% CI = 0.02, 0.96, I 2 = 83%, random effect model]; and in non-RCTs revealed [SMD = 0.09, 95% CI=-0.23, 0.4, I 2 = 82%, random effect model] in students’ knowledge acquisition. Indicating that the observation group was better than the control group in acquiring knowledge (Fig. 2 ). The funnel plot showed that the overall bias was small, with most of the study points falling in the funnel and the upper position and those falling outside the funnel were symmetrically distributed (Supplemental File Fig. 3 ). Additionally, the Egger’s regression test did not exhibit any evidence of publication bias among in total, RCTs and non-RCTs (all P > 0.05). The results of a sensitivity analysis showed that when each study was eliminated one by one, the results did not change greatly. As such, the results were not greatly affected and were relatively robust (Supplemental File Fig. 4 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Forest plot for knowledge acquisition, compared with a control group 2) Before and after comparison. A total of 28 articles reported differences before and after learning, and 11 had more than one observation group. Thus, 50 comparison groups were used to analyze the difference in knowledge between the observation and control groups. The results showed that SMD was 2.15 (95% CI = 1.36, 2.94) [ I 2 = 97%, in the random effects model], which indicated that the medical students acquired knowledge better after the teaching than before the teaching (Fig. 3 ). The funnel plot showed that the overall bias was small, with most of the study sites falling in the funnel and the upper position, and the distribution of the study sites falling outside the funnel was slightly uneven (Supplemental File Fig. 5 ). The Egger’s regression test demonstrated evidence of publication bias ( P < 0.05). The results of the sensitivity analysis showed that when each study was eliminated one by one, the results did not change greatly. As such, the results were not greatly affected and were relatively robust (Supplemental File Fig. 6 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Forest plot for knowledge acquisition—before and after comparison Gain of confidence Assessed through self-reported changes in confidence levels, pre- and post-intervention, using Likert-scale questionnaires or similar tools. This could encompass feelings of preparedness, self-efficacy, and comfort in applying the learned knowledge or skills. Because there were few studies with the control group in the confidence analysis, it was not suitable for meta-analysis. Therefore, only the results of students’ confidence before and after receiving teaching intervention were presented in this analysis. A total of 7 articles reported a difference in students’ confidence before and after receiving relevant learning, and 4 had multiple observation groups; thus, 16 comparison groups were used to analyze the difference in knowledge between the observation and control groups. The results showed that SMD was 5.81 (95% CI = 2.88, 8.75) [ I 2 = 97%, in the random effect model], which indicated that the confidence of medical students increased significantly after receiving relevant teaching (Fig. 4 ). The funnel plot indicated that some publication bias occurred, and the funnel point distribution was not very symmetric (Supplemental File Fig. 7 ). Furthermore, the Egger’s regression test demonstrated evidence of publication bias ( P < 0.05). The results of the sensitivity analysis showed that when each study was eliminated one by one, the results did not change greatly. The results were relatively robust and were not greatly affected (Supplemental File Fig. 8 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Forest plot for confidence—before and after comparison Satisfaction with knowledge Measured through validated questionnaires or surveys that assess the participant’s perceived satisfaction with the educational program’s content, structure, and delivery. It can include items related to relevance, comprehensiveness, and usefulness of the information provided. In the analysis of satisfaction, few studies compared satisfaction before and after the intervention. Only the comparison results of two groups of satisfaction are presented in this analysis. A total of 4 articles reported a knowledge difference between the observation and control groups. The SMD was 0.27 (95% CI=-0.47, 1.01) [ I 2 = 89%, in the random effects model]; subgroup analysis revealed in RCTs SMD=-0.04 (95% CI=-0.50, 0.43); and in non-RCTs revealed SMD = 0.37, (95% CI=-0.63, 1.37), [ I 2 = 92%, in the random effect model] in students’ satisfaction with knowledge. Indicating no difference in satisfaction between the two groups (Fig. 5 ). Owing to the small number of included studies and the small sample size, the funnel plot showed a certain publication bias; the distribution of funnel points was not symmetric and all points fell at the bottom of the funnel (Supplemental File Fig. 9 ). Additionally, the Egger’s regression test did not exhibit any evidence of publication bias between total and non-RCTs (all P > 0.05), however, the Egger’s regression test could not calculate in RCTs for only one article. The results of the sensitivity analysis showed that the results changed greatly when excludeding the study by Shields [ 25 ], suggesting that this particular study had a greater influence on the satisfaction analysis outcomes (Supplemental File Fig. 10 ). Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Forest plot for satisfaction, compared with a control group Discussion Overall evaluation and innovation points This study systematically summarized 41 articles published over the past decade, focusing on the influence of musculoskeletal education on the learning of rehabilitation. By exploring the importance of anatomy in medical students’ learning [ 8 ], the research was narrowed to examining the effects of musculoskeletal studies specifically on the learning outcomes of medical students majoring in rehabilitation, which is relatively innovative. Through a systematic retrospective analysis of existing studies, influencing factors were identified,, and a meta-analysis was used to quantify the impact of these factors. The degree of knowledge acquisition and self-confidence played key roles in musculoskeletal education. In the conducted analysis, students showed obvious differences in knowledge acquisition before and after comparison. knowledge acquisition also showed differences between RCTs and non-RCTs. Indicating that the assessment of teaching quality was more likely to be different before and after students received education. Similarly, after musculoskeletal teaching intervention, medical students’ self-confidence was also improved. Only with sufficient learning self-confidence can they lay a theoretical foundation for subsequent learning. However, there was no significant difference in student satisfaction, which may have been influenced by the insufficient sample size, perhaps indicating that medical students do not have high expectations of musculoskeletal teaching. Regarding specific educational tools and methods, the study considered online musculoskeletal simulations, prosection practices, peer evaluations, and podcasts. Online simulations provide a flexible and accessible way for students to practice and learn about musculoskeletal structures and functions, offering a realistic and interactive experience. However, their quality and realism can vary, and technical issues may hinder their use. Combining online simulations with live demonstrations or hands-on practice can yield better results. Prosections, or cadaver dissections, offer a detailed view of musculoskeletal structures but can be costly and time-consuming to prepare, and may not be accessible to all students. When used in conjunction with lectures or online resources, they can provide a comprehensive learning experience. Peer evaluations can promote collaboration and provide feedback, but may not always be accurate or reliable. They are best used as a supplementary tool. Podcasts offer a convenient way for students to learn on their own time, but the quality and accuracy of information can vary. Using podcasts in conjunction with textbooks or online simulations can provide a more comprehensive learning experience. Overall, the effectiveness of these tools and methods may depend on how they are integrated into the overall educational strategy. Clinical/practical significance Studies have shown that Australian interns have insufficient knowledge of musculoskeletal medicine, and medical students have a tight teaching schedule. However, limited time is often allocated to undergraduate teaching in musculoskeletal diseases [ 57 ]. This may require a review of undergraduate medical education. Less than 2% of the time currently allocated to musculoskeletal disorders [ 58 ]. Another study showed that participation in a two-week clinical elective significantly increased musculoskeletal knowledge among fourth-year medical students, but this alone did not increase clinical confidence [ 59 ]. There was data to show that medical students were not confident in their musculoskeletal knowledge, with no difference between longer and shorter courses, but confidence was significantly higher among students who had completed orthopaedic clinical rotations or other orthopaedic experience [ 60 ]. It can be imagined that the lack of knowledge acquisition and self-confidence was detrimental to both doctors and patients, and most doctors are not satisfied with their musculoskeletal education, only 29% of doctors think that they have enough musculoskeletal education after completing the basic course. 80% of doctors believe they are not educated enough about musculoskeletal [ 61 ]. Recovery from in-person health care has been further complicated by the COVID-19 pandemic [ 62 ]. Rehabilitation after COVID-19 is becoming increasingly pronounced, among which telemedicine has become a hot topic [ 63 ]. However, in the case of remote rehabilitation diagnosis and treatment of patients, this increases the need for rehabilitation doctors. Thus, training qualified rehabilitation doctors is imminent, and rehabilitation teaching is particularly important. Musculoskeletal education is the first course for learning rehabilitation therapy [ 8 ]. In 1999, the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education recognized a set of competencies required of every practicing physician. Professionalism, patient care, medical knowledge, interpersonal and communication skills, systems-based care, and practice-based learning and improvement are the six core competencies [ 64 ]. The improvement of musculoskeletal teaching has improved the six core competencies of medical students to some extent, providing a foundation for clinical practice. This study illustrates the positive impact of musculoskeletal education in the rehabilitation profession. For teachers, teaching tasks can be arranged from the following three aspects: improving knowledge refinement by, providing interesting, vivid, and practical knowledge; encouraging students to improve their confidence in learning professional knowledge; and teaching students to follow their aptitude to meet the knowledge requirements of different students. Musculoskeletal education is more difficult than other disciplines. Therefore, students must improve their self-confidence and dare to explore and acquire this kind of knowledge. Strengths and limitations The strengths of the meta-analysis are multifaceted. Firstly, it constitutes the inaugural comprehensive synthesis to meticulously evaluate and compare the effectiveness of musculoskeletal education in enhancing medical students’ learning of rehabilitation medicine, while also delving into the correlation between various influencing factors and the educational outcomes. This comprehensive approach, to the knowledge, is unprecedented. Secondly, to ensure the robustness and reliability of the findings, the authors conducted rigorous sensitivity analyses, which provided further validation to the conclusions. It is imperative to acknowledge several limitations inherent in the study. From the perspective of literature selection, studies beyond June 2023 to the present were not included. Further, the search was limited to only English articles. In the confidence and satisfaction analysis, the funnel plot demonstrated that a specific bias in the included literature was observed, indicating that the sample size of the included studies was not enough and heterogeneity may exist. During literature retrieval, with the search term “rehabilitation,” few relevant articles were found. Therefore, the selection of teaching intervention methods and research subjects can only be done through subjective exclusion, At the research level, some teaching interventions have brief durations, non-standardized content and quality, small sample sizes, and instances of errors. Conclusions This study conducted a systematic retrospective study on the selected literature and concluded that the influence of musculoskeletal education on physical medicine and rehabilitation students was primarily knowledge acquisition, self-confidence, and satisfaction. Additionally, a meta-analysis of these three aspects concluded that after the intervention of musculoskeletal education, physical medicine and rehabilitation students had significantly improved knowledge acquisition and self-confidence. The inclusion criterion for the literature required that the outcome indicators be the six core competencies. However, this requirement is often overlooked in actual teaching and practice. Future research should prioritize the improvement of these competencies in medical students and provide a theoretical basis for medical teaching. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (1.3MB, docx) Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions YJ contributed to the conception and design of the systematic research and the acquisition, analysis, and interpretation of the data; she performed the meta-analysis substantially revised the article, and contributed to the interpretation of the data. LQM drafted the first version of the article. JZ contributed to the conception and design of the research and the acquisition and analysis of the data. BX contributed to the conception of the work, and the interpretation of the data and substantially revised the article. AF and HS contributed to the manuscript revision. The author(s) read and approved the final manuscript. Funding This study was funded by The Second Affiliated Hospital of Zhejiang University School of Medicine (grant no. 20220213). Data availability The questionnaire used and datasets analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate No ethics approval was needed for this research. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Harkins P, Burke E, Conway R. Musculoskeletal education in undergraduate medical curricula-A systematic review [J]. Int J Rheum Dis. 2023;26(2):210–24. 10.1111/1756-185X.14508. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Lewis R, Gómez ÁCB, Rayman M et al. Strategies for optimising musculoskeletal health in the 21(st) century [J]. BMC Musculoskeletal Disorders. 2019, 20(1): 164 – 78. 10.1186/s12891-019-2510-7 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 3. Lydon S, Fitzgerald N, Gannon L, et al. A randomised controlled trial of SAFMEDS to improve musculoskeletal radiology interpretation [J]. Surgeon. 2021;19(6):386–93. 10.1016/j.surge.2020.11.004. [ DOI ] [