INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7194,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10471787','Statistical and Methodological Considerations for Randomized Controlled Trial Design in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation.','Thakur B, Ayers GD, Atem F, DeClercq JJ, Jain NB.','2023','adaption apprises tidier manualization revoke hte hte hte hte quantiles hte sisaqol adjusts dichotomizing dichotomization dichotomizing conveys computations guesses computations crossovers inexorable untestable mcar mcar hypothetically mcar unobserved mnar nonignorable mcar mcar unobserved mcar mcar mcar mcar mcar mcar mcar mcar impute mcar unobserved unobserved mnar mnar mnar mnar mnar tipping imputations mnar tipping mnar tipping unintelligible exchangeable intricately dsmb fiduciary trumps hariton locascio shiell hawe smeets verbunt tiwpf 0269215505cr923ed applegate graney elam akins nejm199005313222205 bakitas hegel cocreham fiatarone nejm199406233302501 whellan ambrose villareal gurney dieting nejmoa1616338 deyle nejmoa1905877 kitzman whellan nejmoa2026141 austevoll hermansen fagerland spondylolisthesis nejmoa2100990 cd001920 cd001920 longford neugebauer margraf 006x persch ajot minns sackley disulfiram howick tidier e1003294 coupe cd006073 cd006073 kernan viscoli makuch brass steyerberg klaveren k4245 k4245 tanniou tweel teerenstra roes athey imbens recursive laan polley article25 dahabreh dyw125 davidoff m16 vijan kravitz braslow 378x agoritsas varadhan nitsch gracz s67 s77 beurskens koke aja farias e20210306 sperber s124 piedade ferretti maffulli kyte mercieca bebber blazeby bottomley barer sherbourne spritzer schalet egbewale breukelen ogburn rosenblum biom alosh bretz huque vickerstaff ambler latif daud amadera pimentel pimentel fregni fregni 0b013e3181bc0bbd bagiella losina ranstam schnitzer oarsi joca loscalzo follmann rja lavori shera salter fairclough twisk jwr rouanet helmer dartigues jacqmin gadda speckman ellenberg demets herson demets ravaud piaggio elbourne levack wmm engkasan','Abstract Well-designed randomized-controlled clinical trials assessing treatments in the field of physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R) are essential for evidence-based patient care. However, there are challenges unique to clinical trials in PM&R due to complex health interventions in this field. We highlight routinely encountered empirical challenges and provide evidence-based recommendations on statistical and methodological approaches for the design and conduct of randomized controlled trials. Some of the issues addressed include challenges with blinding treatment groups in a rehabilitation setting, heterogeneity in treatment therapy, heterogeneity of treatment effects, uniformity in patient-reported outcome measures, and effect on power with varying scales of information. Further, we discuss challenges with estimation of sample size and power, adaption to poor compliance with treatment and missing outcomes, and preferred statistical approaches for longitudinal data analysis. Keywords: Research Methodology, Evidence-Based Practice, Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Randomized Controlled Trial, Guideline, Research Design, Rehabilitation Research, Scientific Experimental Error, Patient Outcome Assessment Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) are the “gold standard” for high-quality evidence in medical interventional studies. 1 , 2 Randomization reduces bias when examining the cause-effect relationship between treatment and outcome, and remains the only study design that can do so. The field of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation (PM&R) apprises complex health interventions 3 , 4 because many interventions in PM&R are not discrete treatments or actions but a longer process of care or therapy. 5 – 7 Due to this complexity of interventions, the conduct of clinical trials in PM&R remains a challenge. Some highly cited landmark trials in PM&R are well documented. 8 – 17 The interventions in these studies are aimed at improving patient function and health-related quality of life. However, trials in PM&R have methodological limitations including the unavailability of adequate sample size for either the primary endpoint or subgroup analysis, subjective stratification, poor adherence to the treatment regimen and crossover to usual care, lack of blinding, recruitment of patients from a single-center, missing data, and lack of standard assessment tools. These drawbacks limit the generalizability of clinical trials. Additional complexities such as heterogeneity in treatment therapy 18 and heterogeneity of treatment effects, 19 recall bias in outcomes measures, 20 , 21 and lack of required pilot data to design the trial 17 are barriers to designing standard experimental studies. Bias resulting from inadequately designed and powered RCTs poses challenges to the transition from eminence-based medicine to evidence-based medicine in PM&R. The goal of this article is to highlight routine statistical and methodological challenges and provide recommendations that investigators may consider when designing RCTs in PM&R. Clinical Trial Challenges and evidence-based recommendations Blinding of treatment groups in a rehabilitation setting Blinding is an essential step in any prospective randomized controlled trial. It is widely endorsed to reduce bias on outcome measures caused by knowing status of the treatment/placebo intervention. It also helps to improve generalizability of study findings. Because of their frequent use in PM&R studies of modalities, sham therapies to blind participants should be included to fully develop the attempt to achieve a “double-blinded” design when possible. However, blinding in PM&R experimental studies is challenging since physicians and patients can easily distinguish between the two types of rehabilitation therapies given the inherent nature of such treatments. 22 For instance, when comparing physical therapy to placebo, the active intervention (physical therapy) would be self-evident to the patients and the providers. Few mechanisms to improve blinding in rehabilitation trials include blinding of assessors/data collectors, and statisticians analyzing the data. An outcome assessor should be trained to use objective outcome measures whenever possible and be blinded to the study hypothesis, study purpose, allocated treatment group, and knowledge of the treatment that the patients receive. 23 , 24 Also, the outcome assessor should have limited access to other study-related personnel or materials, and should not have access to study data, password-protected databases, and any ongoing intervention-related discussion, which could compromise blinding. 23 , 25 The principal investigator may limit the interaction among study participants, outcome assessors, and healthcare providers to obtain a fair cause-effect relationship. Additionally knowing the success of blinding is important. And therefore, previously suggested Bang’s ‘blinding index’ (BI) 27 , and James’ BI methods 28 could be used to measure the success of blinding. Both methods ask study participants to state whether they believe that they received the experimental active intervention. Reporting of these tests for blinding is also added to the TIDieR checklist in 2020. 29 Heterogeneity in treatment therapy Observed variations in delivery of intervention is known as heterogeneity in treatment therapy which influences the cause-effect relationship. This is especially an issue with rehabilitation therapies. As opposed to drug trials where the drug formulation, dosing, and frequency is well-defined, rehabilitation therapies have heterogeneity. For instance, even the best efforts to standardize physical therapy treatment will result in heterogeneity that is often necessary to follow standard of care to address patient impairments. During the design phase of a study protocol, investigators can draw such knowledge by performing either an original investigation or a systematic review and meta-analysis followed by a sensitivity analysis based on intervention/therapy related parameters in pilot trials. Similar interventional reviews with sensitivity analysis are well documented elsewhere. 18 , 30 Manualization of the protocol, training of those who deliver treatment, and monitoring treatment compliance are additional ways to reduce heterogeneity in delivery of interventions. In addition, cluster randomization is a design technique that can assess/account for variation due to clinicians/sites in which subjects are nested to see if it is of concern. Heterogeneity of treatment effects The randomization process is expected to balance the treatment assignment between the groups by randomly distributing the known and unknown participants’ background characteristics (not a time-varying covariates) which revoke the participants’ and/or investigators’ choice of treatment to provide an average treatment effect. However, groups assigned via the randomization process are not completely similar. Randomization typically results in groups that are different only within a margin of error on specific measured variables. Distinct characteristics of individuals create heterogeneity among the study participants and hence the treatment effect can be modified and misleading. 31 Due to such heterogeneity, treatment benefit for an individual patient may deviate from the reported average effect size. Differential response to the same treatment by different patients or group of patients are known as heterogeneity of treatment effects (HTE). A priori subgroup analysis is the most common analytical approach for HTE assessment. Selection of subgroups should be based on clinical judgement on the prior knowledge of effect modifiers and whether the subgroups are more homogeneous than the total cohort. However, subgroup analysis to assess HTE requires a priori sample size calculation since the sample size to compute the overall average treatment effect is not generally sufficient to provide precise HTE estimates. Additionally, subgroup analysis usually compares group of patients based on a single-variable such as sex or ethnic status, whereas individual patients differ from one another across many attributes simultaneously. Hence such an analysis is useful but it only provides limited information on the variability in treatment effects. 33 , 34 An alternative statistical recommendation is to develop a multivariable risk prediction model that estimates the relationship between several background characteristics and outcome, and provides a risk score for every patient. Patients can be categorized into different risk quantiles (wider range of prognosis), and the treatment effect within each quantile can be estimated by comparing the study outcome measures. 33 , 35 Other approaches to address HTE are effect modelling 33 and machine learning methods. 36 , 37 Additional information on treatment’s effect variation across patients can be found elsewhere 33 , 38 – 44 . Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) A patient-reported measure is a report of the patient’s health condition that comes directly from the individual patient 45 . Such measures provide investigators with the opportunity to monitor progress, treatment planning based on what the patient is experiencing and therefore PROMs are recognized as important endpoints. However, PROMs are limited by potential response bias in RCT’s if patients’ are unblinded to their treatment group 46 and could be a more frequent case of PM&R. Uncertainty in scale-based patient-reported outcome measures may lead to reduced statistical power in clinical trials. Selected PROMs instruments must be validated, relevant to study objectives, and be acceptable to regulatory organizations and the scientific community 47 – 49 . There are 16 essential items (the SPIRIT statement for PRO) 50 that should be added in a trial protocol to improve reporting of PROs. Likewise, the Standards of Reporting Trials Statement-PRO extension (CONSORT-PRO) 51 is recommended for manuscript publication. The International standard consortium guidelines on Setting International Standards in Analyzing Patient-Reported Outcomes and Quality of Life Endpoints Data (SISAQOL) 52 describes methodology standards for data analysis. Outcome Scales and Power Continuous outcome measures such as Short Form 36 (SF-36) 54 and PROMIS-29 v2.0 55 , it is critical to assess a patient’s baseline status (baseline score on the respective outcome measure) and adjust accordingly in the analysis. Such baseline-controlled design is statistically more powerful since it allows quantification of the actual treatment effect. However, the statistical properties of covariate adjustment are poorly understood. For analytical purposes, an approach includes comparing difference of outcome scores from baseline to follow-up between experimental and control groups using analysis of variance (ANOVA) or an independent t-test. Such ANOVA-based test examines only the change in variance of the outcome to control the effect of intervention. However, analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) additionally adjusts the linear effect of continuous covariates on the outcome. Evidence suggests that analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) with baseline continuous outcome measure as a covariate is more powerful with continuous outcomes in RCTs when treatment is assigned through randomization process and provides less bias and more precise results 56 , 57 Such linearity based ANCOVA method could also be used as a sensitivity analysis to validate the study findings. Recent evidence suggests that ANCOVA analysis is still valid when the linear model is arbitrarily mis-specified and provide more precise and valid confidence interval without model assumption. 58 Categorization of continuous variables is common in biomedical research and may help data presentation. However, there are some serious drawbacks. Dichotomizing the continuous variables leads to the reduced statistical power to detect its relationship with other variables due to loss of information and may increase the risk of a positive result being a false positive. Another issue with dichotomization could be underestimation of the extent of variation in outcome between comparative groups. Individual study subjects close to the threshold cut-point but belonging to the opposite sides of that cut-point are characterized as being different rather than being similar. Creating an ordinal variable (using multiple categories) is usually preferred compared to just dichotomizing it. With four or five categories, the loss of information could be small, but that would bring complexities to the analysis. Analysis of continuously measured parameters does not lose information whereas loss of information is quite low with the ordinal parameters compared to dichotomized parameters. Another time-tested approach when reducing scale information by categorization, can be made at the study design stage, is to include repeated measures beyond pre- and post-test to increase degrees of freedom and, other things being equal, statistical power. If there are multiple outcome measures of interest, a primary outcome should be specified as a priori . If the investigators wish to assess multiple outcomes, adjustment for type 1 error is needed to adjust false statistical significance findings. 59 , 60 Investigators should discuss the clinical relevance in addition to statistical significance. A p-value based statistical significance testing can only provide a dichotomous interpretation of study findings that may cause overestimation or underestimation of results. A confidence interval-based approach better conveys uncertainty about the statistical result (estimated effect size) and whether the result is relevant to clinical practice. Complexities in sample size computation According to the CONSORT guideline, reporting sample size computation in a randomized controlled trial is required. Adequate sample size increases the likelihood of detecting a meaningful effect size. Clinical trials in PM&R lack adequate sample size computations 61 , leading to small scale trials 61 , 62 that are inadequately powered and may fail to detect the true clinical treatment effect. A challenge in sample size calculation is the lack of background information on effect sizes from published evidence. Therefore, investigators estimate sample size based on certain assumptions, best guesses, and expert recommendations, without scientific justification. This leads to underestimation or overestimation of sample size. 63 A systematic review has highlighted the reporting of sample size computation in PM&R clinical trials. 61 The following steps are recommended and can be adapted according to the choice of research questions, trial design, and availability of prior evidence. It is essential to determine the primary outcome in a study with more than one outcome to compute the sample size based on the primary outcome. 64 There can be more than one primary outcome. Sample size must be addressed for an interaction term if the study is designed to evaluate the heterogeneity of treatment effects by time or other parameters. The sample size could be estimated at the subgroup level to assess the treatment efficacy (heterogeneity assessment) in the stratified group. If feasible, adequacy of statistical power and estimation precision for secondary endpoints based on the sample size derived for the primary endpoint comparison should be analyzed. Prior to performing a RCT, a feasibility study followed by a well-designed pilot study is recommended. Data from the pilot study could be used to estimate the sample size and the dropout rate in a particular treatment group for the actual RCT. Expert opinion and public/patient surveys on the potential dropout rate on a given treatment could be part of the feasibility study and should be documented to use in an optimum sample size estimation. Such implementations may lower type-II error throughout the trial. Interim analyses (IA) satisfy ethical issues associated with continuing trials based on strong early signals of efficacy by a treatment or lack thereof, protecting patients against ineffective therapies. IA affect both types of inference error and must be planned before the study to understand the effect on sample size. Numerous strategies for concluding early futility or efficacy are available and the literature on these methods is vast. If reliable sample size estimates cannot be obtained, simulation-based sample size computations are recommended. 65 Poor compliance with treatment and missing outcomes Previously presented blinding issues and choice of treatment in PM&R settings shows lack of uniformity on the adherence to treatment. This results in poor compliance rate or higher proportions of crossovers and dropouts in trials which compromises the causal effect sought in a randomized clinical trial. In such situations, interventions to facilitate optimal adherence should be the prime focus during the trial design phase. 66 The investigators should attempt to design strategies to counter reasons for poor treatment compliance. Strategies to improve treatment compliance include informing patients in advance about expectations from the study intervention, 67 and adequate financial incentives to off-set treatment costs and their efforts to participate in the trial. Communication with the study participants can also increase adherence to treatment and improve follow-up rates. However, investigators need to carefully balance this with bias that may result from provider/research team interactions with the patient. Compliance rates should be compared in the intervention groups after final data collection to assess if the differential non-compliance occurs. It is important to document and investigate the reasons for loss to follow-up. Examining the distribution of baseline characteristics between the patients who completed the treatment with those who dropped out within each treatment group may provide investigators an opportunity to identify the known factors associated with the poor treatment compliance. Investigators should be cautious when using statistical models and inferring treatment effect in such situations. An inexorable feature of clinical trials is that the validity of analyses relies on untestable assumptions about the probability of missing data. Rubin’s classification for missing data describe the relationships between variables and their probability of being missing 68 . Data are said to be missing completely at random (MCAR) if missing data on a given variable is unrelated to the variable itself or other variables. MCAR data can be thought of as a random sample of hypothetically complete data. MCAR is a strict assumption that many argue is unlikely in practice. Data missing at random (MAR) represents a less stringent assumption and is actually not missing at random at all. MAR data arise from systematic missingness correlated with other observed study related variables but not necessarily unobserved. Finally, the probability of data missing not at random (MNAR), or data nonignorable nonresponse, is systematically related to the missing data themselves. An example in PM&R arises using income variables 68 , 69 . Patients with lower income may be more likely to not respond to income related questions. A time to missing event analysis in a longitudinal trial design may help provide data on missing completely at random (MCAR) or missing at random (MAR). MCAR is independent of the observed and unobserved data 68 and therefore MCAR assumptions have no impact on the bias. In such a situation, most of the statistical approaches that only include complete cases (e.g., without explicitly modelling the missingness) are valid where the missing data mechanism can be ignored. Assumption of MCAR can be assessed via appropriate bivariate statistics by comparing the distributions of the observed characteristics between the group of treatment adhered and not adhered and confirming that there is no significant difference. Little’s MCAR test null hypothesis that missing data are MCAR may support inference. 70 This chi-squared test tests whether a significant difference exists between the means of distinct missing-value patterns. A p-value of <0.05 associated with Little’s MCAR test is commonly interpreted as the missing data is not MCAR. 70 This test is even valid without normal assumptions but most relevant when the variables are quantitative. Fuchs C. has recommended appropriate chi-squared based large sample test of MCAR assumption when the variable are categorical. 71 Dropping observations when the outcome is MCAR, may have an insignificant effect on the bias of estimation but will reduce precision. 68 However, this assumption may be unrealistic in most situations. 68 A well-documented standard approach can be used to impute the data even when the missing data mechanism is MCAR since it is always best to consider the entire dataset. 72 A MAR is independent of unobserved data, but it depends on observed data 68 and the missing data mechanism is referred to as missing at random (MAR) 68 . 73 If the missing data mechanism is unknown and depends on unobserved data conditional on observed data, it is referred to as missing not at random (MNAR). 68 Presently, there is no approach to produce unbiased estimates when the missing data mechanism is MNAR. 68 Although multiple imputation (MI) 68 was recommended in the past for MAR and MNAR, such imputation should be carried out separately in each of the randomized group. 74 In case of missing data for longitudinal patient-reported outcomes, it is strongly recommended to start the primary analysis assuming the data are MAR and then to use the three approaches of MNAR models for sensitivity analysis to assess the study findings. 75 The three approaches for MNAR models are pattern mixture, selection models, and shared random effects (common parameters) models. 75 Another method, a tipping point analysis is a multiple imputations technique under MNAR assumption and has recently gained popularity 76 . Under this analysis, a tipping point can be defined as the difference of means for continuous data and the difference of event numbers for binary data where the p-values get changed. This method does not require assessing the missing data mechanism, does not involve model uncertainty and assumptions, and is more understandable to the non-statistician community. 76 Analyses using the above methods could estimate the bias and precision in presence of the poor treatment compliance rate with non-informative missing data. However, in both the MNAR model approach and a tipping point analysis, substantial missing data would affect the trial results dramatically and make the results unintelligible. Study findings in such cases should be interpreted cautiously. Additional detailed information on statistical analysis with missing data can be found elsewhere. 68 All these missing data methods presented above can be implemented within the standard framework of generalized linear models with extensions for longitudinal data analysis using mixed models or generalized estimating equations to incorporate repeated measures correlation. Mixed-effects model versus generalized estimating equation The mixed-effects model is an extension of simple linear model that uses fixed effects and random effects which allows regression coefficients to vary between subjects. 77 A generalized estimating equation is a complimentary method of modeling where we assume a correlation structure which estimates the average treatment response of the population (“population-average” effects). 77 Both methods, mixed-effects model estimated by maximum likelihood and marginal models estimated by GEE are standard and highly suitable for the analysis of longitudinal or clustered data 78 because the adjustment of repeated measure within a subject is made in both approaches. 77 We recommend both methods be applied to the same data to make more complete inference. Population averaged models (GEE) probably should not be used to make inferences about individuals and mixed models are perhaps less useful making inferences about populations, even though population estimates can be derived from the mixed model). These models have the following benefits: A random intercept model is a specific case of the mixed-effects model where subjects deviate in terms of both intercept and slope and it is empirically the same as GEE with exchangeable correlation 77 . However, unlike the random-effects model, the GEE will provide consistent estimates of the slope even if the variance model is mis-specified. Mixed-effects analysis allows subject effects to vary. GEE approach needs a larger sample size compared to mixed effects model due to its robustness. However GEE models may converge where the mixed model does not in smaller sample sizes. Mixed-effects model have the flexibility to evaluate the need of random slopes from a likelihood ratio test. 77 When data can be assumed to be missing at random, the mixed model provides robust estimates via maximum likelihood estimation whereas GEE does not. Above points suggest choosing the mixed-effects model to analyze the longitudinal data, however, convergence of such models is not guaranteed in general. GEE is a useful alternative when a maximum likelihood solution is not available. 77 Additionally, a GEE analysis carries more flexibility in terms of modelling different correlation structure which is not available in mixed-effects model analysis. Recently some of the statistical software packages provided flexibility to add additional correlation structure in the mixed-model analysis. The rationale behind adding additional correlation term in the mixed effects model is, regardless of the adjustment carried by random coefficients the “error” is still correlated within individual subjects. 77 Additional considerations A goal of any interventional study is to generate evidence based upon the clinical significance of the treatment. Therefore it is essential to focus on the magnitude of a treatment effect that would impact or change evidence-based practice. 79 Examples from the PICO (population, interventions, comparator, outcomes) criteria are available for additional reading. 79 An experienced statistician is a critical member of a clinical research team, especially in the setting of RCT’s. Unfortunately, their expertise is not optimally utilized during study design and conduct of the trial. Statisticians should be involved from early stages of a project including generating a research question, planning of the study design, generating the statistical analysis plan, and protocol design. A statistician should be intricately involved during conduct of the clinical trial and in the final analysis and interpretation of data leading to a publication. A data safety monitoring board (DSMB) made up of outside experts (typically of 3 to 10 members), play a critical role to monitor the participant’s rights, safety, and treatment efficacy during an ongoing trial. 80 , 81 Such a board also serves in an advisory capacity to the trial leaders and the study sponsors. They promote best practices to address the emerging challenges during the conduct of the trial. 82 Planning a multi-center study, collaboration between community and academic partners, and developing a fiduciary relationship with the patients is essential to building a concrete research infrastructure to execute RCTs in PM&R. The specific challenges related to nonpharmacological trial such as in rehabilitation treatments is not adequately considered in the CONSORT statement. Hence, an extended version of the CONSORT guideline is encouraged 83 . Additionally, a guideline on the selection of comparators, a range of possibilities for reporting of standard care, reporting of intended mechanisms of control, and reporting a rationale for the hypothesized margin is recommended for noninferiority and equivalence designs 84 , 85 . A similar guidance on the selection of comparators for superiority trial design is lacking. 85 After completion of the RCT, findings should be published as a peer-reviewed manuscript. Efforts towards dissemination of findings should be made. Conclusions and future directions RCT’s in the field of physical medicine in rehabilitation are challenging due to the inherent nature of the interventions in these settings. We have presented common methodological and statistical issues in the design, analysis, and reporting of results of RCTs in PM&R, and made recommendations to meet these challenges. Footnotes Author Disclosures: There are no conflicts of interest among the authors and individuals in control of the content of this article. REFERENCES 1. Barton S Which clinical studies provide the best evidence? The best RCT still trumps the best observational study. BMJ. Jul 29 2000;321(7256):255–6. doi: 10.1136/bmj.321.7256.255 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hariton E, Locascio JJ. Randomised controlled trials - the gold standard for effectiveness research: Study design: randomised controlled trials. BJOG. Dec 2018;125(13):1716. doi: 10.1111/1471-0528.15199 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Shiell A, Hawe P, Gold L. Complex interventions or complex systems? Implications for health economic evaluation. BMJ. Jun 7 2008;336(7656):1281–3. doi: 10.1136/bmj.39569.510521.AD [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Wade DT, Smeets RJ, Verbunt JA. Research in rehabilitation medicine: methodological challenges. J Clin Epidemiol. Jul 2010;63(7):699–704. doi: 10.1016/j.jclinepi.2009.07.010 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Multiple Sclerosis: National Clinical Guideline for Diagnosis and Management in Primary and Secondary Care. 2004. National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence: Guidance. [ PubMed ] [ G