INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7201,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11734040','Entry-to-Practice Rehabilitation Competencies and the Rehabilitation Competency Framework: A Gap Analysis.','Beamish NF, Cunningham S, Footer C, Lowe R.','2024','rcf orthotics rcf rcf orthotics rcf rcf rcf scopes rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf orthotics orthotics rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf caaspr caaspr ccup cacce wfot wfot orthotics orthotics ispo orthotic ispo arn arn arn caaspr caaspr ccup cacce rcf rcfs rcfs rcfs rcfs rcfs rcf slp rcf discharging orthotics slp rcfs rcf slp rcf orthotics slp rcfs rcf slp rcf orthotics slp rcfs rcf slp rcf orthotics slp rcfs rcf slp rcf orthotics slp operationally scopes rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf nondisabled rcf rcf rcf rcf rcf positionality rcf rcf commonality rcfs unmapped rcf nonfinancial physiopedia usaid relab relab usaid causey kamenov chatterji amatya groote conradie berner louw 2020a aretz 2020b yaqoob jabri kvist azimirad turunen dawkins renwick ensor shinkins jayne meads hwf caaspr 2018a caaspr caaspr 2018b caaspr iaap iupsys iaapsy ref_competences_iaap_2016 wfot wfot competency_final wpt orthotics ispo ispo orthotics cdn ymaws ispoint resmgr 3_learn ispo_standards_nov2018_sprea arn arn rehabnurse arn_rehabilitation_nursing_competencymodel_final_ _may_2014 batt tavares beamish footer regalado icr scarrow schellenberg bhattarai morean rep0000321','Abstract Objectives To systematically map the entry-level competencies of rehabilitation professions to the World Health Organization''s Rehabilitation Competency Framework (RCF) to identify overlapping commonalities and gaps across available rehabilitation frameworks. Design The competence frameworks of audiology, occupational therapy, physical and rehabilitation medicine, psychology, physiotherapy, prosthetics and orthotics, rehabilitation nursing, and speech and language therapy were researched online. In cases where international standards or competencies were unavailable online, expert colleagues in the related field were consulted to confirm the absence of an international document. A nationally recognized and freely available document was selected to represent the profession if no international document was found. The frameworks were then mapped to the RCF domains. Setting Desk-based research. Participants Not applicable. Interventions Not applicable. Main Outcome Measures Not applicable. Results The professional documents demonstrated a stronger alignment with the competencies outlined in the RCF activities. The practice and professionalism domains showed the greatest congruence with profession-specific competencies, whereas the learning and development, management and leadership, and research domains had varying levels of alignment. This consistency in mapping may be attributed to the profession-specific competencies’ focus on the fundamental entry-level knowledge, skills, values, and abilities essential for delivering safe and effective patient care. Conclusions The mapping exercise revealed that competencies in the profession-specific frameworks primarily focused on individual-level skills for effective patient care rather than societal-level impact, such as acting as rehabilitation advocates. The study provides valuable insights into the alignment between profession-specific competencies among the rehabilitation professions. Identifying commonalities and gaps can facilitate the development of shared educational resources for foundational support across diverse rehabilitation disciplines. This effort can contribute to building a robust and unified rehabilitation workforce capable of meeting the emerging health needs of diverse populations worldwide. Keywords: Clinical competence, Competency-based education, Competency framework, Interprofessional, Rehabilitation Rehabilitation has emerged as a critical health strategy for the 21st century, as emphasized by the World Health Organization''s (WHO) “Rehabilitation 2030: Call for Action.” 1 The rising prevalence and associated consequences of noncommunicable diseases, aging populations, and health emergencies emphasize the demand and importance of a substantial rehabilitation workforce. 2 Despite the demand for rehabilitation services, there exists a substantial shortage in the rehabilitation workforce, which is particularly evident in low and middle-income countries (LMICs), where only a fraction of individuals in need can access services. It is estimated that only 3% of the individuals who require rehabilitation can access the service in LMICs, 3 with <10 rehabilitation practitioners per one million population in LMICs. 1 In comparison, it is estimated that high-income countries have approximately 40 times more therapists than LMICs. 4 It is evident that there is an urgent need for workforce development initiatives to help support individuals and rehabilitation practitioners in LMICs. The rehabilitation workforce is diverse, with multiple professions and specializations that collectively meet populations’ needs. The WHO identifies 8 core professions that offer rehabilitation services: audiology, occupational therapy, rehabilitation specialists in medicine, clinical psychology, physiotherapy, prosthetics and orthotics, rehabilitation nursing, and speech and language therapy. 5 Each rehabilitation profession has developed its own international or national framework. Competency frameworks guide the training, assessment, and professional development of practitioners, ensuring that they are equipped with the knowledge, skills, and interpersonal attributes to provide high-quality patient care and help to establish individual and service-wide development priorities. 6 Although diversity in the rehabilitation workforce is necessary and valuable, it can present a particular challenge to lower-resourced countries and settings attempting to educate, regulate, and sustain a diverse workforce with varied expectations. 7 The WHO developed the Rehabilitation Competency Framework (RCF) to address this challenge. The RCF acts as a reference tool for creating contextually based competencies for the rehabilitation professions. 8 Organized thematically into domains of practice, professionalism, learning and development, management and leadership, and research, the RCF aims to communicate the expected performance of the rehabilitation workforce. 7 The scopes of practice among rehabilitation professionals often overlap, indicating areas of shared competencies that play a pivotal role in health care transformation, fostering innovation, and facilitating role integration. 9 However, numerous factors, such as fluctuations in the health care workforce, disparities in access to health care services because of geographic and economic reasons, community health needs, emerging health concerns of local populations, and guidelines for education, training, and examination, can influence the competencies required of rehabilitation professionals. 6 , 10 , 11 By identifying core competencies across the rehabilitation professions, the RCF provides a blueprint for ensuring rehabilitation professionals have the competencies to deliver quality care across the scope of rehabilitation. 8 The RCF was developed with an interprofessional perspective and, therefore, can also serve as a framework to compare entry-level competencies across the professions. Identifying shared competencies can inform innovative learning models to drive workforce development by combining the potential of interprofessional collaborations and shared content development that are designed to meet local rehabilitation needs. Interprofessional collaborations and shared resources could potentially maximize the use of limited resources to support the education of rehabilitation professionals and assist in meeting workforce needs in LMICs. Although Mills et al 7 state that a content analysis of select existing competency frameworks was conducted when developing the RCF, it is unclear what frameworks were used. The researchers also did not explore how closely the developed RCF competencies aligned with each rehabilitation profession''s competency frameworks for entry-level professionals. Therefore, the purpose of this study was to systematically map the unique entry-level competencies of the rehabilitation professions to the RCF to identify commonalities and gaps across professions. Methods Rehabilitation professions competency document search An internet search was performed to acquire competence frameworks for the different rehabilitation professions, including audiology, occupational therapy, physical and rehabilitation medicine, psychology, physiotherapy, prosthetics and orthotics, rehabilitation nursing, and speech and language therapy. A gray literature search plan was developed to incorporate 4 different search strategies: gray literature databases, Google search engines, targeted websites, and consultation with experts in the profession. Search terms included competency framework, competence framework, educational framework, standards of education, standards of practice, and scope of practice for each identified rehabilitation profession. Separate searches were conducted for international educational frameworks for each of the professions by the authors between January 2021 and February 2021 and again in November 2023 to ensure the most up-to-date competency frameworks were used. A competency framework was included if (1) it was published by an international association or representative of the profession, (2) it was available in English, (3) it was the most current version of the document, and (4) it addressed professional practice. In addition, the purpose of the document and the target audience was considered. Exclusion criteria included (1) the document was a draft or summary version or had been replaced with a more recent version, (2) the document did not include competencies for a single rehabilitation profession, and (3) the document was not endorsed by an international association or regulatory agency for the profession. The following sites were searched for international standards: World Physiotherapy, World Federation of Occupational Therapists, International Society of Physical and Rehabilitation Medicine, International Society for Prosthetics and Orthotics, International Association of Applied Psychology, and International Union of Psychological Science. Web pages from each search were screened until 3 consecutive pages elicited no new results. When international standards or competencies were unavailable through online searches, expert colleagues in the related field were contacted to confirm that an international document did not exist for the profession. When international documents were not identified, an agreed-upon, published, comprehensive, freely available national document was used to represent the profession. Rehabilitation professional competencies mapping Analysis of the competency frameworks was performed using conceptual content analysis. 12 Each competency was assessed by 2 researchers. Coding of implicit and explicit terms was performed using the RCF domains as a pre-existing code set. NVivo 12 software (NVivo version 12.6.1) a was used for coding. Each rehabilitation profession''s competency framework was then systematically mapped to the competencies and activities within the 5 RCF domains. The documents were not mapped to the RCF skills and knowledge sections because these skills are context-specific to a rehabilitation profession. In contrast, the competencies and activities are considered universal and relevant to all rehabilitation professions regardless of their scope of practice, roles, or responsibilities. The RCF competency or activity was considered “mapped” when most key terms were identified in the professional framework language. If the RCF competencies and activities did not directly map to the competencies described in each profession''s document, keywords for each competence and activity were searched to ensure that associated competencies or activities were not missed. A competency or activity was considered “partially mapped” when some, but not most, key terms were identified in the framework language. A competency or activity was considered “did not map” when there was no clear connection or language in the framework to link it to the RCF. Three members of the research team performed the mapping exercise. The coding of the 3 researchers (NB, SC and CF) was reproducible for 96% of the mapping. When agreement on the competency being mapped was absent, a consensus was reached through consultation with the fourth member of the research team (RL). The maps were then color-coded to facilitate a visual inspection of the mapping outcomes. Due to the nature of the study, IRB approval and informed consent were not applicable. Results International competency framework documents were found for all professions except audiology, speech and language therapy, and rehabilitation psychology. Therefore, 2 professions were represented using national documents, the Canadian Alliance of Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology Regulators 13 , 14 competency profiles. The International Declaration on Core Competences in Professional Psychology 15 was the only international document identified for the psychology profession. Competencies specifically for rehabilitation psychology were not located at a national or international level, and therefore, the International Declaration on Core Competences in Professional Psychology was used. The limited mapping of these competencies to the RCF domains may be because of this limitation, and results for psychology should be carefully considered. Details related to the competency frameworks used for the mapping activity can be found in table 1 . 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 Table 1. Guiding document used for each rehabilitation profession. Profession Framework Derivation Domains Audiology National audiology competency profile 13 , 14 Completed in 2018 in collaboration with 22 subject matter experts from across Canada, which included representatives from CAASPR, CAASPR member jurisdictions, Speech-Language and Audiology Canada (SAC), the Canadian Academy of Audiology (CAA), the Canadian Council of University Programs in Communication Sciences and Disorders (CCUP–CSD), and the Canadian Academic Coordinators of Clinical Education (CACCE). 1. Expert (knowledge and clinical expert) 2. Communicator 3. Collaborator 4. Advocate 5. Scholar 6. Manager 7. Professional Occupational therapy Minimum standards for the education of occupational therapists 16 Revision from the 2002 standards. A review process was coordinated by the World Federation of Occupational Therapists (WFOT) Education International Review Team in consultation with Member Organizations, WFOT Executive and council and international stakeholders 1. The person-occupation-environment relationships and the relationship of occupation to health and well-being 2. Therapeutic and professional relationships 3. An occupational therapy process encompassing collaborative, people-centered, occupation focused processes 4. Professional reasoning and behavior 5. The context of professional practice Physical and rehabilitation medicine Core curriculum & competencies for the professional practice of physical and rehabilitation medicine 17 International Society of Physical and Rehabilitation Medicine education committee with stakeholder involvement through an online survey. 1. Patient safety and quality patient care 2. Medical knowledge and procedural skills 3. Interpersonal skills and communication 4. Practice and systems-based learning 5. Reintegration of people with disabilities into society 6. Medical ethics and public health 7. Quality assurance 8. Policies of care and prevention of disabled people 9. Professionalism Physiotherapy Physiotherapist education framework 18 Guidelines were informed by World Physiotherapy''s policy statements and guidelines with input from the member organizations of World Physiotherapy. 1. Physiotherapy assessment and intervention 2. Ethical and professional practice 3. Communication 4. Evidence-based practice 5. Interprofessional teamwork 6. Reflective practice and lifelong learning 7. Quality improvement 8. Leadership and management Prosthetics and orthotics International Society for Prosthetics and Orthotics (ISPO) education standards for prosthetic/orthotic occupations 19 Standards were developed using a 5 step approach, which included (1) surveying current members and educators, (2) focus groups, (3) literature review, (4) review of current position, and (5) created a list of recommendations for education standards that was used to consult key stakeholders and was then endorsed by the ISPO Executive Board. 1. Entry-level personnel 2. Institutional/program requirements 3. Program curriculum 4. Faculty 5. Program admission Psychology International Declaration on Core Competences in Professional Psychology 15 Framework was established through content analysis of current national models, in consultation with relevant stakeholders from 18 countries. 1. Psychological knowledge and skills underpinning the core competencies 2. Professional behavior competences 3. Professional activities competences Rehabilitation nursing Association of Rehabilitation Nursing (ARN) competency model for professional rehabilitation nursing 20 Developed by an ARN task force of experts representing clinical and academic settings developed an evidence-based framework to guide professional rehabilitation nursing practice. Charged by the ARN Board of Directors to accomplish this strategic objective, the panel discussed, debated, and reviewed all facets of modern rehabilitation nursing. The resulting model was validated by member review and comment and approved by the Board of Directors 1. Nurse-led evidence-based interventions to promote function and health management in persons with disability and/or chronic illness 2. Promotion of health and successful living in persons with disability or chronic illness across lifespan 3. Leadership 4. Interprofessional care Speech-language pathology National speech-language competency profile 14 Completed in 2018 in collaboration with 22 subject matter experts from across Canada, which included representatives from CAASPR, CAASPR member jurisdictions, SAC, CAA, CCUP–CSD, and CACCE. 1. Expert (2 subcategories: knowledge expert and clinical expert) 2. Communicator 3. Collaborator 4. Advocate 5. Scholar 6. Manager 7. Professional Open in a new tab The results of the mapping exercise are displayed in Table 2 , Table 3 , Table 4 , Table 5 , Table 6 . The competencies from each RCF domain mapped more consistently than the activities to the profession-specific competencies. Overall, the RCFs practice domain (domain 1) and professionalism domain (domain 2) demonstrated the greatest congruence with all profession''s competencies. RCFs practice domain (domain 1) and professionalism domain (domain 2) demonstrated the greatest congruence with all profession''s competencies. Audiology, physiotherapy, occupational therapy, speech and language therapy, and nursing documents closely mapped to the RCFs practice. However, not all professions mapped to practice competencies related to patient-centered care. All professions demonstrated close alignment with most competencies in the RCFs professionalism domain. There was less congruence with the learning and development (domain 3), management and leadership (domain 4), and research (domain 5) domains, and therefore, it appears that entry-level practice may not reflect these advanced competencies with education focused on practice and professionalism needed for clinical practice. Table 2. Mapping to the RCFs domain 1: practice. RCF AUD OT PRM PSYCH PT P&O RN SLP Mapped Competencies to the RCF (%) Domain 1: Practice competencies C1. Places the person and their family at the center of practice 