hing the required outcome of training.” 21 The uniqueness of the scopes of practice and the practice context across and within professions may have resulted in the overall limited mapping to the activities outlined in the RCF. The profession-specific competency frameworks focus on the minimum level of competence for a novice practitioner. They are only intended to be a resource for education and professional practice, because they are not validated and do not transfer to the dynamic and complex rehabilitation needs of local communities. 5 , 22 They are often used as the blueprint for developing the core curriculum for entry-level education, which may explain why rehabilitation professions’ competencies map closely to the RCF practice and professionalism domains. The consistency in mapping to these domains, including both competencies and activities, may provide education programs with an opportunity to share resources for instruction with the potential overlap of the underlying knowledge skills and abilities required for the safe performance of the activities within the competency. Strengthening education and training ensures that rehabilitation professionals have the knowledge, skills, and resources necessary to provide high-quality rehabilitation services to patients. Rehabilitation is a complex process that requires an interprofessional approach. Although the foundational knowledge and skills vary among the rehabilitation professions, shared competencies include (1) evidence-based clinical practice knowledge and skills, (2) culturally competent communication and collaboration, (3) professional reasoning and behaviors, and (4) interprofessional collaboration. 23 Acknowledging these shared competencies provides an opportunity to pool resources to provide instruction and training to rehabilitation professionals. Furthermore, providing interprofessional instruction may also promote the rehabilitation professions’ understanding of the commonalities and differences in professional expectations to work as a competent team. Three domains (learning and development, management and leadership, and research domain) had competencies that did not consistently map to the rehabilitation professions’ competency documents outside of the need to perform evidence-based practice. These competencies could be viewed as abilities that experienced rehabilitation professionals possess, such as (1) working to strengthen rehabilitation education and training, (2) strengthening the evidence for rehabilitation, and (3) acting as a rehabilitation advocate. 1 A strength of the RCF is the use of a level system within each competency and skill to capture the diverse scope of practice within the rehabilitation workforce. 7 The RCF defines each competency and activity using a 4-level system to demonstrate the growth of a professional''s proficiency, autonomy and clinical decision-making skills. 7 There may be a potential lack of alignment between the RCF and rehabilitation professions competency documents in the learning and development, management and leadership, and research domains because each profession''s document was created to define the entry-level competencies for new practitioners. This lack of alignment highlights the importance of competencies and activities that support the growth of the rehabilitation workforce that may be considered beyond entry-level education. The learning and development domain includes continuing professional development (CPD) as well as supporting the learning of others. To provide effective rehabilitation services, health care professionals need a strong understanding of the underlying principles of rehabilitation and current evidence. Although the underlying principles of rehabilitation are often considered a component of entry-level education, implementing evidence-based practice requires professionals to participate in CPD to maintain current expertise and is a component of knowledge translation. Knowledge translation is an iterative process involving professionals and patients synthesizing, disseminating, exchanging, and ethically applying knowledge to improve health by developing tools, guides, and practical recommendations. 24 Knowledge translation can play a crucial role in reducing the gap between research and practice. 25 Most activities within the research domain focused on the performance and dissemination of research. It is important to note that most of the rehabilitation research has been conducted in high-income countries. The challenges faced by LMICs in providing rehabilitation services differ from those of high-income countries. Research to identify innovative and cost-effective rehabilitation services that meet the challenges of limited resources, inadequate infrastructure, and a shortage of health care professionals is needed to improve the health outcomes of individuals with disabilities in these settings. Equipping young professionals with the skills to perform research would allow for creating evidence that aligns with the culture of care and resource capabilities within the context. The competencies in the professional documents focused on providing quality patient care by working in interprofessional teams to implement evidence-based practice. The focus on competencies has been on the individual level versus the societal level. Only one profession, speech-language pathology, fully mapped to the competency “acts as a rehabilitation advocate.” This finding may reflect the need for rehabilitation practitioners to shift focus from the patient to the population level to promote equity for persons with disabilities. 26 According to Khan et al, 3 health equity is necessary because of inequalities between disabled and nondisabled individuals in various areas, including access to health care, health-related behaviors, exposure to unhealthy environments, and insurance coverage. 3 Rehabilitation providers have not traditionally worked in the field of public health despite the communal goal of assisting patients in fulfilling their societal roles and integrating fully within the community. The WHO views disability and functioning as resulting from the interplay between health conditions and contextual factors. Research has shown that social and built environments play a significant role in the existing health disparities between persons with a disability and persons without a disability. 27 As a result, these disparities are largely preventable. There is an opportunity for rehabilitation professionals to leverage their foundational principles to promote health equity in disability communities. 26 The future promotion of health equity in disability by rehabilitation professionals depends on appropriate education and training of future generations. Audiology, physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and speech and language therapy are closely mapped to the RCF competencies across all domains. Physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and nursing are also mapped or partially mapped to most activities. A potential reason for this may be that 50% of the RCF technical working group consisted of occupational therapy, physiotherapists, nurses, and speech and language pathologists. 7 Therefore, the language used within the documents could have been more similar to those used in these professions. Another explanation for this discrepancy may be that 67% of the participants in the first round of the Delphi study conducted by Mills et al 7 during the creation of the RCF were from these professions. As Mills 7 describe in the limitation section of their manuscript, despite their best efforts, there needed to be an equal balance in their demographics, including the professions represented, which may have affected the results. 7 Potentially, the effect of the professions represented in the creation of the RCF may have resulted in the learning and development domain being more reflective of the guidelines of these professions. Although consistency was noted across the practice and professionalism RCF domains and activities, mapping the curricula of the rehabilitation education programs across multiple contexts and country income levels may provide an improved understanding of global education for rehabilitation professionals and highlight additional opportunities to support the development of resources to support entry-level education. Moreover, conducting a comprehensive global assessment to examine the access and demand for CPD among interested rehabilitation partners could provide valuable insights for enhancing education among practicing professionals beyond entry-level practice. This endeavor may be particularly beneficial in aligning CPD initiatives with the learning and development, management and leadership, and research domains. By assessing the existing CPD options and identifying additional needs across the rehabilitation professions, it becomes possible to facilitate the sharing of resources and knowledge, thereby enhancing efficiency in professional development efforts. Study limitations There are 2 significant limitations to the mapping exercise that are worth noting. First, the authors involved in the analysis are all physiotherapists from high-income countries. Therefore, confirmation bias may have occurred when reviewing the documents because of the research team''s positionality. We are acutely aware that this work may be overly influenced by our backgrounds as physiotherapists, which may question the utility and validity of our findings among other professions. To improve our mapping utility and validity, the map and frameworks for each profession could have been provided to active members in each rehabilitation profession in multiple countries globally to ensure that all relevant information was captured. The second potential limitation is the use of national frameworks from a high-income country. To try to capture a global perspective for each profession, international documents were used because they are believed to reflect the accreditation standards of each profession regardless of geographic location. However, an international document only existed for some professions. Without international competencies, the frameworks used for audiology and speech-language pathology chosen were created by the Canadian Alliance of Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology Regulators. 13 , 14 As a result, these professions may only be representative of the Canadian context. Similarly, the Association of Rehabilitation Nursing 20 created the framework used for rehabilitation nursing, which is based in the United States. Therefore, this report may heavily reflect the views of North America and not be reflective of other countries or cultures. Conclusions The purpose of this study was to systematically map the unique entry-level competencies of the rehabilitation professions to the RCF to identify commonalities and gaps across professions that present opportunities for the development of shared resources for foundational educational support across the disciplines. The 8 rehabilitation professions appear to encompass the first 2 domains of the RCF: practice and professionalism. The commonality of the competencies and activities within these domains may represent areas for shared resources among education programs to support workforce development. However, not all rehabilitation professions directly map to the RCFs competencies in the domains of learning and development, management and leadership, and research. Further research is needed to identify rehabilitation professionals’ agreement with the unmapped RCF competencies and activities and the perceived CPD needs of rehabilitation professionals across the globe. This research may provide greater insight into the resources needed to meet essential competencies required to continue developing the rehabilitation workforce to help support individuals needing rehabilitation services. Supplier a. NVivo, version 12.6.1; NVivo 12 