INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7223,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12309834','Innovations In Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation: Advances in the Diagnosis, Treatment, and Care of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis.','Arnold WD, Castoro R, Saxena S.','2025','riluzole edaravone tofersen fals pma pbp diplegia diplegia fals fals fals spinocerebellar chitinase chitotriosidase chit1 chitinase chi3l2 ykl39 chit1 chi3l2 chit1 chit1 chi3l1 mislocalizes mislocalization biofluids escorial collaborates riluzole edaravone riluzole edaravone scavenger slows tofersen qalsody intrathecally tofersen fals nct04768972 jacifusen ind141673 afinersen anticipates orthotic walkers wheelchairs wheelchairs orthotic walkers wheelchairs wheelchairs adapts exoskeletons dysarthria bci neurorestorative pictured nextgen castoro nextgen smita nextgen wda dyne insmed myotonic theratech chemotherapeutics innomedica 510x dimachkie diplegia cnd 0b013e31829e22d1 diplegia goizet soulages vallat rusina vandenberghe bruffaerts diagnostics11040624 moglia tardbp e208082 parobkova matej degenerations diagnostics11030509 morren pioro araki behler ludolph kassubek ijms24031911 pezeshgi ms9 pdff fnins jamali kethamreddy burkett hhg araki tateishi basophilic alowais alirezaei s12035 chitinase chitinases ubiquitinated noto amyotroph edaravone traynor corr hardiman korobeynikov lyashchenko redondo jafar nejad shneider sinaki gubbay bf00313868 chio neurologica tb00892 punia ajpm kudritzki porciuncula s220 s232 ezzat blink','Abstract Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive motor neuron disease that causes loss of upper and lower motor neurons, leading to muscle weakness and ultimately death. This review highlights recent advancements in Neuromuscular Medicine and Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (PM&R), emphasizing innovations in the diagnosis, treatment, and care delivery for ALS. The field of PM&R emphasizes a multidisciplinary, patient-centered approach, incorporating advanced diagnostic tools, therapeutic strategies, adaptive equipment, and telerehabilitation to optimize function. Neuromuscular PM&R physicians play a key role in managing symptoms and maximizing functional independence. Current disease-modifying therapies include riluzole and edaravone which provide only modest benefits, but emerging gene therapies like tofersen for SOD1-related ALS offer promise for targeted treatment for genetic forms of ALS. Future advancements in regenerative therapies, biotechnologies, and digital health integration hold the potential to improve care and enhance the quality of life and functional independence of individuals living with ALS. Introduction Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (PM&R) emphasizes improving functional abilities rather than a specific disease or organ systems. Physiatrists (PM&R specialists) optimize function through a multidisciplinary approach, combining therapies, medical interventions, and adaptive solutions for comprehensive, patient-centered care. Neuromuscular medicine, a subspecialty of both PM&R and Neurology, focuses on hereditary and acquired disorders of the peripheral nervous system, including motor neuron diseases, peripheral neuropathies, neuromuscular junction disorders, and myopathies. Motor neuron diseases include a diverse group of disorders that lead to the loss or impairment of spinal and/or cortical motor neurons, resulting in compromised motor function. Neuromuscular physiatrists play a vital role in enhancing quality of life and functional independence by addressing complex physical, respiratory, and functional challenges through specialized rehabilitation strategies and multidisciplinary care. This role becomes especially critical in progressive motor neuron diseases, where physiatrists employ tailored rehabilitation approaches and collaborate with a multidisciplinary team to support patients’ quality of life and maintain functional independence at each stage of disease progression. This review will examine advancements in Neuromuscular Medicine and PM&R, emphasizing diagnostic, therapeutic, and adaptive innovations in managing amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). It will explore current standards of care, recent innovations, and future advancements in diagnosis, treatment, and care delivery, including developments in diagnostic technologies, therapeutic strategies, adaptive equipment, and telerehabilitation. We will review the evolving therapeutic landscape, including disease-modifying therapies and emerging gene therapies that target specific genetic forms of ALS, highlighting the critical need for specialized clinical settings to deliver and monitor these treatments. Overall, this review aims to illustrate how these advancements collectively work toward enhancing quality of life and optimizing functional outcomes for individuals affected by ALS. Advances in Diagnostic and Monitoring Technologies ALS has an estimated incidence of 1.4/100,000 with an age of onset typically occurring in the sixth or seventh decade of life. 1 However, the incidence of ALS in the Midwest is higher than national average and has been estimated at 8.8 per 100,000. 2 ALS includes both hereditary (familial) and sporadic forms, with up to 10–20% of sporadic ALS patients (those with no apparent family history) also found to carry a mutation associated with familial ALS (fALS). The PM&R neuromuscular specialist plays a crucial role in the diagnosis and management of a diverse range of disorders that impair function. The diagnosis of a motor neuron disease, specifically ALS, is achieved through a combination of history and physical examination, and diagnostic testing. A distinctive feature of ALS is the degeneration of both upper and lower motor neurons with relatively sparing of other neurons. ALS can present with a range of variant forms ( Figure 1 ). For instance, primary lateral sclerosis (PLS) involves only upper motor neurons, while progressive muscular atrophy (PMA) affects only lower motor neurons. Regional ALS variants include progressive bulbar palsy (PBP), which impacts bulbar muscles; brachial amyotrophic diplegia (BAD), affecting proximal upper limbs; and leg amyotrophic diplegia (LAD), involving distal lower limbs. 3 , 4 Phenotypic variability in fALS, even among cases with the same genetic mutation, suggests that ALS variants are diverse manifestations of a common underlying pathology, though the basis of this phenotypic variability across patients remains unclear. Notably, some patients with ALS may also exhibit additional overt neurological features, such as frontotemporal dementia ( Figure 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Variant Forms of Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis. Figure created using BioRender. Diagnosis of ALS Optimal treatment and rehabilitation for ALS relies on rapid and accurate diagnosis, as well as assessment of disease impact. Unfortunately, ALS is often associated with diagnostic delays which can postpone the initiation of disease-modifying treatments, supportive care, and rehabilitation. ALS is diagnosed clinically based on findings of combined upper and lower motor neuron involvement, supported by diagnostic tests that confirm the diagnosis or exclude the diagnosis. Diagnostic approaches include electrodiagnostic testing (discussed in McClellan et al. in this issue), which assesses lower motor neuron involvement while ruling out mimicking disorders. While the diagnosis is usually straightforward in advanced cases, it can be more challenging early in the disease course or in patients with atypical ALS variants. Advances in Diagnosis To promote earlier diagnosis and improve outcomes in ALS, current research focuses on refining diagnostic criteria, and scoring systems, and developing emerging biomarkers, imaging techniques, and electrophysiological tools. Advances in understanding the genotype-phenotype relationship in fALS have aligned clinical, pathological, and genetic findings within families, revealing distinct patterns of disease presentation, predominantly linked to autosomal dominant (AD) inheritance. 5 , 6 Patients with fALS associated with frontotemporal dementia (FTD) exhibit multisystem involvement, collectively termed ALS-plus syndromes. 7 – 9 These include cognitive and neurobehavioral impairments alongside motor symptoms. Such patients frequently present with bulbar-onset ALS and have a higher prevalence of pathogenic gene mutations or expansions, often leading to shorter survival compared to those without ALS-plus syndromes. On average, diagnosis of ALS takes 10–16 months from symptom onset, primarily due to unfamiliarity with the disease, incomplete symptom recognition, and delayed specialist involvement. 10 Advances in ALS diagnostics focus on advanced imaging and neurophysiological techniques to assess upper motor neuron involvement, which traditional electrodiagnostic tests cannot, as well as developing blood and liquid biomarkers. Advanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) techniques, including diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) and functional MRI, may help detect white matter disruptions and cortical dysfunction associated with upper motor neuron impairment in ALS. 11 , 12 Ultrasound and fat fraction-based MRI techniques of the muscles have also been utilized to identify sub-clinical/early stages of muscle degeneration. 13 , 14 Additional imaging technologies, like positron emission tomography (PET) and single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT), may provide improved insights in ALS patients. 15 These technologies have helped to identify and establish a series of degenerative events occurring in ALS before overt symptoms. 16 The first pattern is marked by a decline in motor function over five years, marked by anterior horn cell loss and corticospinal tract degeneration. A second pattern, clinically similar to the first, also shows postmortem involvement of the posterior columns, Clarke’s column, and spinocerebellar tracts. The third pattern shares features with the second but includes a prolonged survival period exceeding 10–20 years. 16 – 18 Moreover, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are beginning to be utilized to analyze extensive datasets from electronic medical records, genetic profiles, and imaging studies. 11 , 19 These AI-driven approaches hold promise for enhancing diagnostic precision, identifying ALS sooner, and predicting disease progression, which could optimize treatment timing. For example, AI may help clinicians determine the optimal point to initiate supportive therapies, like non-invasive ventilation, facilitating personalized, timely interventions that match each patient’s needs for optimized outcomes. Together, these diagnostic advancements are setting the stage for more effective and targeted ALS management strategies, improving both the timing and impact of therapeutic interventions. Advances in Disease Tracking Beyond early diagnosis, effective ALS management also requires reliable methods to track disease progression and assess responses to therapies. One of the most advanced biomarkers for this purpose is neurofilament light chain (NfL), a blood-based indicator of neuronal and axonal damage used across various neurological conditions. 20 Similar to how elevated creatine kinase (CK) signals muscle damage, increased NfL levels in the blood suggest neural injury. NfL lacks specificity as it rises in response to any neural injury and can be influenced by factors like age, limiting its utility as an early diagnostic marker. However, NfL remains promising for monitoring disease progression and therapeutic effects in ALS. Additionally, emphasis has been on non-neuronal cells such as activated microglia and astrocytes which promote chitinase expression. Thompson et al. showed that chitotriosidase-1 (CHIT1) and chitinase-3-like protein 2 (CHI3L2/YKL39) could differentiate ALS from mimicking conditions, but their diagnostic performance was weaker than neurofilament. CHIT1 and CHI3L2 were correlated with the rate of disease progression, and CHIT1 was correlated with survival when included in a multivariate model. 21 A longitudinal study consistent with previous findings showed that CHIT1 and CHI3L1 were linked to the rate of disease progression. 21 , 22 Advancements in electrophysiological methods are also improving our ability to track ALS progression. High-density surface electrodes now enable non-invasive recording and analysis of individual motor units, allowing more sensitive detection of motor neuron health over time. Additionally, techniques like transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) offer non-invasive assessment of motor cortex function, providing insight into central nervous system and upper motor neuron integrity. In approximately 97% of ALS cases, TAR DNA-binding protein 43 (TDP-43) mislocalizes from the nucleus to the cytoplasm in affected glial cells and neurons, disrupting critical RNA processing, splicing, and transport functions. 23 , 24 Recent research has identified promising cerebrospinal fluid and blood biomarkers associated with TDP-43 mislocalization, including altered splicing events and cryptic peptides, which show potential for both earlier diagnosis and tracking of disease progression 25 . Notably, the distribution of the pathological isoforms of TDP-43 has been well established from human postmortem tissue and biofluids. Studies employing enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) to quantify TDP-43 in the CSF, and plasma of different patient groups identified significant differences between sporadic ALS patients and a group of neurological and healthy control subjects. 26 Higher TDP-43 levels over the 95% CI to control subjects, correlated with ALS-specific early phase of the disease but did not correlate with the level of diagnostic certainty (applying the El Escorial criteria for ALS) nor with disease onset site (bulbar vs. limb). 26 , 27 Overall, there is an urgent need for improved methods that allow earlier diagnosis. This is critical to reduce the diagnostic delay and to facilitate early treatment implementation. Together, these emerging biomarkers and advanced monitoring techniques are paving the way for earlier diagnosis and more precise, real-time tracking of ALS, which is crucial for timely interventions and evaluating therapeutic efficacy. Advances in Treatment and Rehabilitation Interventions The PM&R neuromuscular physician plays a critical role in managing symptoms, enhancing quality of life, and maximizing functional independence for ALS patients. Although there is no cure for ALS, the PM&R physician collaborates with a multidisciplinary team to address issues such as muscle weakness, spasticity, pain, and mobility through individualized rehabilitation programs. These programs often incorporate physical and occupational therapy, adaptive devices for mobility and communication, and strategies to maintain daily function as the disease advances. Additionally, the PM&R physician manages fatigue, respiratory difficulties, and nutritional needs, ensuring comprehensive care tailored to each patient’s progression and specific challenges. Disease-Modifying Therapies Currently, two disease-modifying therapies, riluzole and edaravone, are approved for treating all patients with ALS, offering modest slowing of disease progression. Riluzole extends survival by an average of approximately four months, while edaravone, a free radical scavenger originally developed for stroke, slows disease progression by around 30%. 28 , 29 Most ALS cases are sporadic, but some patients, with or without a family history, have identifiable genetic mutations. For patients with genetic forms of ALS, treatment typically mirrors that of sporadic ALS. However, recent advances are beginning to differentiate treatment for genetic forms of ALS. Tofersen (Qalsody), an antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) therapy, was recently approved for SOD1-related familial ALS. Administered intrathecally, tofersen reduces SOD1 protein levels, which has been shown to lower neurofilament light chain (NfL) levels—a marker of neural injury—through clinical outcomes have varied. Current research is exploring the impact of early or presymptomatic treatment, as these stages may yield the greatest therapeutic benefit. ASOs targeting various other fALS mutations are in clinical trials such as (FUSION; NCT04768972 ) to assess efficacy, safety, and pharmacology of FUS ASO; (jacifusen), or the recent FDA approval of (IND141673) for the testing of the mixed backbone ASO; afinersen in a single C9orf72-ALS participant hold great future promise. 30 , 31 However, specialized clinics are essential to deliver these emerging therapies effectively, allowing for precise monitoring of their therapeutic effects and potential side effects in real-time. Such clinics must be equipped with expertise in genetic counseling, advanced diagnostics, and intrathecal administration techniques, providing patients with comprehensive, tailored care that anticipates the complexities of gene therapies. Supportive Care and Rehabilitation The mainstay of treatment for most patients impacted by ALS includes multidisciplinary supportive and rehabilitative care to mitigate the impact of the disease. Supportive care is delivered in a personalized manner to address the symptoms experienced by the patient. Patients with ALS generally progress through six stages ( Table 1 ), each with common rehabilitation needs, although individual progression can vary. 32 In Stage 1, patients remain fully ambulatory with mild weakness, benefiting from a range of motion and strength exercises for unaffected muscles. Stage 2 patients are still ambulatory but experience moderate weakness, often requiring orthotic devices for foot drop, hand support, and assistance with daily tasks. Stage 3 involves severe weakness in specific muscle groups, with aids like walkers, wheelchairs, and lift chairs to support mobility and socialization. In Stage 4, patients are non-ambulatory but independent, using powered wheelchairs and hospital beds for support, with exercises limited to unaffected muscles. Stage 5 patients require assistance for transfers and mobility, with family education and pressure relief becoming key priorities. Stage 6 involves complete immobility and dependence on skilled caregivers, with an emphasis on positioning and palliative care to maximize comfort. Average survival from symptom onset is between two and three years, while survival after diagnosis averages 15 months, though this can vary widely among patients. 1 , 33 , 34 End-of-life discussions should begin early in ALS management with input from hospice and palliative medicine experts. Table 1. Six Stages of ALS Progression and Common Rehabilitation Needs 32 Stage Patient Characteristics Rehabilitation Needs Stage 1 Fully ambulatory with mild weakness Range of motion and strength exercises for unaffected muscles. Stage 2 Ambulatory with moderate weakness Orthotic devices for foot drop and hand support; assistance with daily tasks. Stage 3 Severe weakness in specific muscle groups Use of walkers, wheelchairs, and lift chairs for mobility and socialization support. Stage 4 Non-ambulatory but independent Powered wheelchairs, hospital beds; limited exercises for unaffected muscles. Stage 5 Dependent for transfers and mobility Family education on care; focus on pressure relief and maintaining comfort. Stage 6 Complete immobility and dependence on skilled caregivers Positioning strategies, palliative care for comfort, and skilled caregiver support. Open in a new tab Advances in Care Delivery and Adaptive Equipment Multidisciplinary Care Effective management of motor neuron diseases like ALS relies on a robust multidisciplinary approach, where PM&R specialists, neurologists, physical therapists, and other healthcare providers collaborate to deliver comprehensive, patient-centered care. This team-based model is essential for addressing the full spectrum of ALS symptoms and ensuring that care remains holistic, adaptable, and supportive throughout the disease’s progression. Telerehabilitation Access to care is a major barrier to optimal clinical outcomes across healthcare and including ALS. In particular, individuals who live in rural areas are impacted by health disparities as compared with individuals who live in urban areas. 35 Telehealth and remote monitoring are transforming rehabilitation for ALS by making care more accessible and responsive. 36 Using wearable devices, clinicians can continuously track muscle activity, mobility, and fine motor skills, allowing for real-time adjustments to rehabilitation programs. 37 These devices capture subtle changes in gait, strength, and dexterity, which enables clinicians to optimize functional outcomes and potentially slow functional decline. Data from wearable sensors on posture, balance, and physical activity support early detection of decline, enabling quick adaptation of therapies, better adherence to home exercises, and personalized care irrespective of location. Precision Rehabilitation ALS presents significant variability in the disease initial presentation and progression as well as treatment responses among individuals. In the future, precision medicine will address this variability by tailoring interventions specifically to each patient, moving beyond the current “one size fits all” approach. Emerging advancements in diagnostic tools, monitoring technologies, and therapeutic strategies are paving the way for precision rehabilitation, a customized treatment model that adapts to the unique needs of each patient. As AI and machine learning capabilities evolve, rehabilitation protocols will become increasingly data-driven, with algorithms adapting treatments based on clinical metrics and biomarkers like NfL. Looking ahead, AI algorithms are expected to allow analysis of patient data to predict treatment outcomes, enabling clinicians to prioritize therapies with the highest probability of success for each individual, ultimately transforming ALS care through personalized, precision-driven approaches. Adaptive and Assistive Technologies Innovations in adaptive and assistive technology are empowering ALS patients to maintain autonomy, communication, and mobility, even as motor functions decline. Exoskeletons, robotic-assisted therapy devices, and customized home modifications create supportive environments tailored to each patient’s needs, enhancing safety and functionality. Integrated diagnostic monitoring tools, such as speech recognition software and swallowing sensors, track the progression of dysarthria and dysphagia in real-time, allowing for personalized speech and swallowing therapies. Advanced assistive technologies, including eye-tracking devices and brain-computer interfaces (BCI), further enable patients to interact with their environment and communicate effectively. 38 , 39 These tools may help sustain independence and improve quality of life by ensuring ALS patients can continue to engage with their surroundings as physical limitations increase. Conclusion, Impact on Clinical Practice, and Future Directions ALS is a life-altering diagnosis with limited treatment options, yet the neuromuscular PM&R specialist plays an essential role in achieving timely diagnosis and coordinating comprehensive, multidisciplinary care. New innovations in diagnostics, disease monitoring, treatment, rehabilitation, and assistive technology offer hope in reducing the disease’s impact on patients’ quality of life. Current efforts aim to improve early diagnosis, tailor disease monitoring to individual needs, enhance rehabilitation techniques, and integrate assistive devices that support mobility, communication, and daily function. Long-term goals remain focused on developing regenerative and neurorestorative therapies, which could potentially alter disease progression or restore function. Footnotes Open in a new tab W. David Arnold, MD, (pictured) is the Executive Director of NextGen Precision Health and a Professor in the Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, University of Missouri-Columbia, Columbia, Missouri, USA. Ryan Castoro, MD, is an Assistant Professor in the Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation and Principal Investigator at the NextGen Precision Health, University of Missouri-Columbia, Columbia, Missouri, USA. Smita Saxena, PhD, is a Professor in the Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, and a Principal Investigator at NextGen Precision Health, University of Missouri-Columbia, Columbia, Missouri, USA. Disclosure: WDA has received grant funding from NMD Pharma, Avidity Biosciences, consulting fees from Avidity Biosciences, NMD Pharma, Dyne Therapeutics, Genentech, Design Therapeutics, Catalyst Pharmaceuticals, and Insmed regarding therapeutic development for Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy, Myotonic Dystrophy type 1, and Spinal Muscular Atrophy. RC has received consulting fees from Theratech for the development of chemotherapy neuropathy limiting chemotherapeutics. SS has received grant funding from Innomedica AG, Novartis, Switzerland. The authors acknowledge the assistance of ChatGPT in refining the manuscript’s clarity and grammar. The final content and interpretations remain the sole responsibility of the authors. References 1. Norris F, et al. Onset, natural history and outcome in idiopathic adult motor neuron disease. Journal of the neurological sciences. 1993;118:48–55. doi: 10.1016/0022-510x(93)90245-t. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Mehta P, et al. Prevalence of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) United States, 2016. Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis and Frontotemporal Degeneration. 2022;23:220–225. doi: 10.1080/21678421.2021.1949021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Dimachkie MM, et al. Leg amyotrophic diplegia: prevalence and pattern of weakness at US neuromuscular centers. Journal of clinical neuromuscular disease. 2013;15:7–12. doi: 10.1097/C